TI  - [SGINC] Cyber Threats in South Asian Mobile Financial Services: A Systematic Review of Fraud Risks, Detection Methods, and Policy Responses (2020–2026)
AU  - Shuvo
PY  - 2026
DO  - https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/nw2ts
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This project is a systematic literature review examining cyber threats, fraud risks, and countermeasures in mobile financial services (MFS) across six South Asian economies — Bangladesh, India, Pakistan, Sri Lanka, Nepal, and Bhutan — between 2020 and 2026. MFS platforms (including mobile money, digital wallets, and interbank payment systems such as UPI) have driven rapid financial inclusion across the region, but this expansion has been accompanied by a proportional rise in fraud incidents, ranging from social engineering and OTP interception to SIM swap fraud, account takeover, identity fraud, and agent-facilitated collusion. The review follows PRISMA 2020 guidelines, searching eight databases (Scopus, Web of Science, IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, PubMed, Google Scholar, ProQuest, and EconLit) to identify peer-reviewed empirical studies meeting PICOS-based eligibility criteria. Included studies are quality-appraised using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (MMAT 2018) and synthesized narratively around four research questions: What cyber threats and fraud typologies are documented in South Asian MFS literature? What technological and organizational countermeasures are proposed or implemented? What regulatory frameworks govern MFS cybersecurity across the region, and how mature are they? What research gaps remain in the South Asian MFS security literature? Expected outcomes: A cross-country comparative mapping of fraud typologies, countermeasure effectiveness (including machine learning-based detection systems), and regulatory maturity across the six countries; identification of geographic and methodological research gaps (particularly the underrepresentation of Sri Lanka, Nepal, and Bhutan); and an actionable research and policy agenda for MFS providers, regulators, and future scholars, including recommendations on authentication design, agent oversight, and regional regulatory harmonization.
