AU  - Mostafizur Rahaman
AU  - Rajib Chandra Das
AU  - Md.Rimon
PY  - 2026
JO  - Psychosocia Journal of Applied Psychology and Social Psychology
DO  - https://doi.org/10.61978/psychosocia.v4i3.1695
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Cyberbullying has become a new form of digital violence that can be dangerous to the safety of individuals, their mental health and social inclusion. Ongoing gender discrimination and power dynamics place women at a particular risk of cyberbullying and other forms of online harassment. The goal of this study is to explore the existing situation and the dominant trends of cyberbullying in Bangladesh, specifically how cyberbullying affects women's cyber-safety and well-being. The study is qualitative secondary data analysis based on relevant academic articles, reports, policy documents and previous studies on cyberbullying, cybercrime, gender-based violence, and digital safety in Bangladesh. The findings reveal that the adoption of social media platforms like Facebook, WhatsApp, Messenger and other digital communication platforms has facilitated an increased ability to engage in harassment, threats, abuse, discrimination and the sharing of harmful content online. Gender-based online violence disproportionately impacts women and can limit their access to online spaces and adversely impact their psychological and social health. Likewise, the study also reveals that low digital literacy, low awareness about online safety, lack of cybercrime law enforcement, and negative attitudes towards gender are the factors that have left cyberbullying practices persistent. The study finds that the implementation of existing legal provisions, digital literacy and awareness building, reporting and support processes, and dealing with gender-based discriminatory attitudes and social norms are the key areas that need to be emphasized to help reduce cyberbullying in Bangladesh. They will play a crucial role in making Bangladesh's digital space safer, more inclusive and gender-responsive.
