AU  - Md Asraful Islam
AU  - Kazi Abdul Mannan
PY  - 2026
JO  - Journal of State Government and Mass Media
DO  - https://doi.org/10.64907/xkmf.v04i04.jsgmm.2
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] AI-assisted impersonation and social engineering make it necessary to distinguish threat recognition from protective practices. This study examines associations between AI-enabled cybercrime awareness and self-reported prevention among law students in Bangladesh. Protection Motivation Theory informs the interpretation of awareness and protective responses. Of 385 survey records, 253 respondents identified as law students and constituted the primary sample. Following reliability diagnostics, awareness and preventive-practice items were analysed separately. Ordinary least squares models used HC3 robust inference, with ordered logistic models, Holm correction and full-sample analyses as sensitivity checks. Among students, 49.0% endorsed recognition of AI misuse for phishing or impersonation, 80.6% reported verifying unusual requests, and 82.2% endorsed security practices. Adjusted awareness coefficients were small and uncertain for both outcomes; all four confidence intervals included zero, and all Holm-adjusted p values were 1.000. Daily AI use was positively associated with verification (b = 0.308, 95% CI 0.115-0.502) and security practices (b = 0.255, 95% CI 0.067-0.444). Ordered logistic models showed a comparable pattern. The findings support distinguishing recognition, perceived detection capability and behaviour-specific responses in cybersecurity research and university education. They identify contextual associations rather than causal effects and highlight the need for validated measures and prospective behavioural assessments. Generalisation is limited by the available recruitment documentation and the self-reported cross-sectional design. Keywords: cybercrime awareness; preventive practices; law students; protection motivation; generative artificial intelligence; Bangladesh; measurement validity
