AB  - [SGMAY: PROTOCOL of a related TFGBV scoping review - check if completed] Technology-facilitated gender-based violence (TFGBV) has become a significant concern in Bangladesh as access to mobile internet and social media has expanded. The Violence Against Women Survey 2024 provided the country's first national estimate: just over 8% of women with access to technology reported lifetime exposure to selected forms of TFGBV, and the figure was much higher among younger women. Smaller studies report considerably higher rates and describe harassment, image-based sexual abuse, impersonation, hate speech and blackmail, mostly on Facebook and messaging apps. Yet the evidence remains scattered across journals, conference proceedings and organisational reports. Existing reviews treat Bangladesh as one country among many and do not examine how the Bangladeshi literature explains this violence or responds to it. This scoping review aims to identify and synthesise evidence on TFGBV against women and girls in Bangladesh and to identify gaps for research, policy and practice. It asks four questions: what forms TFGBV takes and how common it is; what drivers are reported, with particular attention to masculinities and gender norms; what impacts it has on women and girls and how they seek help; and what legal, institutional and technological responses exist. The review follows the JBI methodology for scoping reviews and will be reported according to PRISMA-ScR. We will search ten academic databases and relevant grey literature for sources published in English or Bangla from 2006 onwards. Two reviewers will screen records independently, and the findings will be charted and analysed using qualitative content analysis, informed by the ecological framework and the concept of hegemonic masculinity. The expected outcome is a comprehensive map of what is known about TFGBV in Bangladesh, where the evidence is thin, and how far current laws and services match women's reported experiences. The findings are intended to inform researchers, policymakers and organisations working to prevent violence against women in Bangladesh.
