AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This comprehensive work bridges cybercrime law with the reality of history's most dangerous cyberattacks, creating a unique reference that unites legislative rigor with battlefield dynamics. Spanning six major parts, the book progresses from conceptual foundations (definition, elements, classification of cybercrimes) through legal liability and penalties, criminal procedure and digital evidence, international cooperation and jurisdictional conflicts, detailed case studies of landmark attacks, to emerging threats in the age of artificial intelligence. The work examines transformative cyber incidents including Stuxnet (the first cyber weapon), NotPetya (the most destructive attack), SolarWinds (supply chain espionage), the Bangladesh Bank heist, WannaCry, and Colonial Pipeline, demonstrating how abstract legal principles apply to concrete threats. Four critical additions distinguish this revised edition: analysis of cyber insurance and the ransom payment dilemma under international sanctions; legal protections for ethical security researchers and responsible disclosure; export controls on dual-use surveillance tools under the Wassenaar Arrangement; and a novel framework for cognitive security and neural rights, protecting human cognition from deepfakes and AI-driven attacks. Licensed under CC BY-NC 4.0 for academic use with commercial licensing reserved, this work provides foundational understanding for navigating cybercrime law's past, present, and future across twenty languages and multiple legal traditions.
