AB  - [SGEXC: not cybercrime] Prologue: Ahmed Rajib Haider, 15 February 2013, Dhaka It was on this date that blogger and activist Ahmed Rajib Haider, known by his pen name Thaba Baba, was hacked to death just outside his home. A leading voice in the Shahbag protests and a sharp critic of Islamist politics, Haider had used his blog to advocate for secular justice and to condemn religious fundamentalism. His murder – not the first attack, but the first in what would become a series of brutal killings – marked a turning point in the public visibility and vulnerability of non-religious dissent. The next day, his coffin was carried in a vast public procession to Shahbag Square, draped in the national flag and accompanied by over 100,000 mourners. However, the act of public grieving became a political flashpoint. Islamist groups seized upon Haider's known atheism to brand the entire movement as nāstik – heretical and immoral. What began as a protest for justice swiftly became a referendum on the legitimacy of atheism in public life, setting in motion a period of heightened risk for secular activists. This chapter returns to the enduring reverberations of that moment – to the quieter and more lasting traces left behind after the spectacle receded. Introduction When I revisited Sylhet for a research stint in February and March 2022, I was warmly welcomed by my former research interlocutors and friends.1 Seeing me again after a long time, some of my atheist interlocutors emphasised, ‘Things are very different from when you came before.’
