TI  - [SGINC] The State–Corporate Nexus in Cyber Surveillance and Its Impact on Civil Liberties: A Comparative Study of Bangladesh and India
AU  - Md. Saiful Islam
AU  - Ashiq Iqbal Jishad
PY  - 2026
JO  - World Affairs
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1002/waf2.70088
AB  - [SGINC: reviewer] ABSTRACT This paper examines the state‐corporate nexus of digital surveillance and its impacts on civil liberties through a comparative analysis of Bangladesh and India. It seeks to investigate how the states enable monitoring, censorship, and state‐controlled surveillance by partnering with corporates, enacting strict laws, and securitizing discourses. Private firms in both countries harness state power with new technology to erode privacy and democratic values, and to establish a “controlled” society. Similarly, by introducing laws such as the Digital Security Act (2018) and Cyber Security Act (2023) in Bangladesh, and by implementing digital regulations and Aadhaar initiatives in India, both states justify institutional surveillance within a legal framework. The study also scrutinizes the state‐corporate nexus through securitization theory, the influence of surveillance capitalism, endorsing surveillance through judicial provisions, and the impacts of securitization efforts of states over civil liberties.
