TI  - [SGMAY] Digitalization without Confidence: The Role of Institutional Transparency in Shaping Youth Involvement in Bangladesh's Financial Market
AU  - Ashiqun Nabi
AU  - Hasib Ahmed
AU  - Jannatul Ferdaus
AU  - Asif Abdullah
AU  - Ashrafun Nahar
AU  - Akilama Akter Srity
PY  - 2026
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20483261
AB  - [SGMAY: cyber harm mentioned] The paper focuses on the role of institutional transparency in encouraging the youth to participate in the financial market of Bangladesh amid the high financial digitalization. Despite the increased access to financial activities by mobile banking and online platforms, which are digitized financial services, young people are still underrepresented in formal investment markets. The paper fills this gap by investigating the connection between digitalization, institutional transparency, trust, and youth involvement in a financial market.A mixed-methods research design was identified. The survey questionnaires were used to gather quantitative data and 94 youth respondents provided survey questionnaires and 91 valid responses were subjected to SPSS to conduct analysis. The qualitative data were gathered in a form of semi-structured interviews with 8 participants and analyzed using thematic analysis with NVivo. According to the results of the survey, there is a high representation of digital financial use by young people as 76.6% of the surveyed use digital financial services on a regular basis. Nevertheless, investor confidence is low with only 40.7% of the respondents confident in the financial market of Bangladesh with 35.2% being neutral and 24.2% unconfident. The research also reveals that 70.3 of the respondents said that the absence of transparency makes them reluctant to invest and the majority of 56.1 said that they do not invest because they do not trust.The survey results are corroborated by the interview results which indicate that digitalization has made things more convenient and more accessible but it has not made serious investment engagement. According to the respondents, unclear information, poor accountability, corruption, risk of fraud, market manipulations, and financial illiteracy are some of the greatest obstacles to youth participation. The research finds that meaning youth participation can be guaranteed only through digitalization, but only backed up by institutional transparency, greater regulation, dependable disclosure, investor protection and financial literacy. The research adds to the financial inclusion knowledge, demonstrating that access to digital financial services does not necessarily generate confidence in financial markets. It suggests that financial institutions, the regulators, and educational institutions should collaborate to establish a more transparent, safe, and youth friendly financial sector in Bangladesh.
