AB  - [SGMAY: cyber harm prominent in abstract] Over the past decade, the proliferation of social media platforms has opened new avenues for micro-entrepreneurship in Bangladesh, most significantly through Facebook-based commerce (F-commerce). This study examines the structural challenges and coping strategies of rural women entrepreneurs engaged in F-commerce, with particular attention to how digital opportunities intersect with gendered social structures and informal market conditions across selected districts of northern Bangladesh. Employing a qualitative research design, the study draws on in-depth interviews (IDIs) with 25 rural women business operators and four key informant interviews (KIIs) with individuals holding professional expertise in women’s entrepreneurship and digital business environments. The findings reveal that entry into F-commerce was predominantly driven by income insecurity, particularly in the aftermath of the COVID-19 pandemic, and further facilitated by exposure to other women-led online businesses. While F-commerce reduced entry barriers and enabled flexible, home-based income generation, participants encountered persistent structural challenges. These included delivery-related fraud and customer refusal in cash-on-delivery (COD) transactions, the absence of consumer and seller protection mechanisms, restricted access to formal financial services, and conditional household support shaped by patriarchal norms. The compounding burden of domestic responsibilities and emotional stress associated with online harassment further jeopardised business sustainability. The study concludes that F-commerce functions principally as an adaptive livelihood strategy rather than an assured pathway to economic empowerment for rural digital entrepreneurs. Sustainable digital entrepreneurship for rural women requires structural interventions that extend well beyond digital access and skills training to encompass platform protection, financial inclusion, recognition of gendered labour burdens, and coherent institutional support mechanisms.
