AU  - Tahura Tabassum Tari
PY  - 2026
JO  - Cornerstone (Minnesota State University, Mankato)
AB  - [SGINC: reviewer] This study explores the experiences of Bangladeshi female gamers in online multiplayer gaming spaces, focusing on the marginalizing messages they encounter, the emotional and social impacts, and the coping strategies they use. Although online gaming is often seen as an open and inclusive digital space, for many women it remains shaped by sexism, exclusion, and cyberbullying. In Bangladesh, these experiences are further influenced by patriarchal norms, moral policing, and gendered expectations. Using a qualitative research design, this study employed in-depth semi-structured interviews with Bangladeshi female gamers who actively participate in online multiplayer games, along with cyber-ethnographic observation of gaming-related Facebook groups and online interactions. The data were analyzed through thematic analysis to identify recurring patterns across participants’ experiences. The findings reveal that female gamers frequently encounter sexist jokes, verbal abuse, body shaming, stalking, trolling, exclusion from teams, moral judgment, and financial exploitation. Participants reported emotional distress, anxiety, stress, anger, low self-esteem, and self-doubt, with these effects often extending into offline life and personal relationships. To cope, participants used strategies such as muting voice chat, hiding gender identity, avoiding random teams, blocking users, relying on trusted people, and selective confrontation. The study argues that online gaming spaces reproduce broader patriarchal power structures, forcing women to constantly negotiate between participation and protection. This research contributes to understanding gendered digital harassment in Bangladesh and highlights the need for safer and more inclusive gaming environments.
