AU  - Qazi Moinuddin Mahmud
AU  - Md. Rezaul Karim Shohag
PY  - 2025
JO  - Journal of Management Accounting Governance and Performance
DO  - https://doi.org/10.63817/jmagp.07.2025.015
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Purpose/Objective: This paper explores one of the most common forms of financial crime in Bangladesh, bank loan fraud, which is often not directly addressed in most studies, yet has a significant impact on the country’s banking industry. Loan fraud in banking consists of fraudulent loan applications and approval procedures. Methodology: This qualitative study explored the perceptions of a purposive sample of twenty bank officials working in the credit departments of various banks in Dhaka city. Bank managers, Bangladesh Bank officers, senior bank officers and comparatively young professionals with relevant experiences were covered. Additionally, two fraudulent bank loan applicants were also interviewed using snowball sampling. Findings: This study has found that the motivating factors for loan fraud can be categorised into four major factors: avariciousness, corruption, political patronage and nepotism, and enforcement deficit. The presence of immense greed worked as the psychological microlevel motivator for fraudsters. In addition to the major factors mentioned above, lack of good governance and a culture of malicious competition among banks were found as organisational factors motivating the perpetrators to commit loan fraud. Originality: This work has been inspired to investigate a common problem of bank loan fraud, which lacks similar criminological research and official data in the context of Bangladesh. The perpetrator’s view is a novel addition to the study of loan fraud. Available studies focused on media reports of sensitised loan scams, ignoring the regular phenomenon of loan fraud. This study has tried to explore the problem of loan fraud, an offence that is rarely reported in official public reports. Considering the vulnerability of our banking industry and the deteriorated public trust in it, this study reports a unique view on financial crime in a corrupt developing country. Implications: This research has indicated the drawbacks of the current banking practices, mainly focusing on the cultural aspects, and hence it emphasises corrupt practices in our banking industry. It suggests a unified effort of bank authorities, customers, the government, and other relevant stakeholders to stop loan fraud. For example, maintaining proper guiding principles, maintaining transparency in loan lending practices, increasing bank customer awareness, encouraging training facilities for young professionals in the banking sector, and similar actions can be prioritised.
