ID  - R0200
TI  - [SGMAY] The Dual State: Artificial Intelligence, Authoritarian Control, and the Crisis of Youth in Bangladesh (2014-2024)
AU  - Ashikur Rahman
PY  - 2025
DO  - https://doi.org/10.22541/au.176194054.49910899/v1
AB  - [SGMAY: cyber harm prominent in abstract] This thesis examines the transformative and tumultuous decade in Bangladesh from 2014 to 2024, a period defined by the consolidation of power under Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina and the Awami League, and a parallel socio-technological revolution. It argues that the Bangladeshi state has evolved into a "Dual State": one that projects an image of a "Digital Bangladesh" driven by economic growth and technological adoption, while simultaneously operating as an increasingly authoritarian regime that leverages these very technologies for surveillance, control, and the suppression of dissent. The paper analyzes this duality through three interconnected lenses: the strategic deployment of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and digital tools for state security and public narrative control; the systematic quashing of student-led political movements and protests; and the resultant societal decay manifesting in a public health crisis, rising suicide rates, and criminality. Central to this analysis is the phenomenon of the "Sheikh Hasina AI Detector," a purported technological tool that symbolizes the regime's attempt to weaponize AI for political purposes. The thesis concludes that this decade has created a profound crisis among Bangladesh's youth, caught between the state's promise of a digital future and the grim reality of political suffocation and social despair. Keywords: digital authoritarianism, Sheikh Hasina AI Detector, Bangladesh student protests, Digital Security Act (DSA) 2018, mental health crisis Bangladesh, competitive authoritarianism, political repression, youth despair, bot networks astroturfing, Dual State BangladeshChapter 1: Introduction – The "Digital Bangladesh" Paradox The period from 2014 to 2024 stands as a definitive era in the history of Bangladesh. Marked by the unprecedented political dominance of Sheikh Hasina and the Awami League, this decade witnessed the realization of the party's flagship vision: "Digital Bangladesh." Initiated in 2009, this vision promised to leverage information and communication technologies (ICT) to propel the nation into a developed, knowledge-based economy. By the mid-2010s, the fruits of this policy were increasingly visible: widespread mobile internet penetration, a burgeoning IT sector, digitization of government services, and a growing sense of national pride in technological advancement. However, beneath this veneer of progress, a more sinister reality was taking shape. The general elections of 2014, boycotted by the main opposition Bangladesh Nationalist Party (BNP) and its ally Jamaat-e-Islami, were marred by widespread violence and critically low voter turnout, granting the Awami League a virtually uncontested parliamentary supermajority. This electoral victory, while legally securing a term, fundamentally eroded the legitimacy of the democratic process and set the stage for an era of de facto one-party rule. The 2018 elections further cemented this trend, being widely criticized by international observers as deeply flawed. This thesis posits that these two narratives—the triumphant march of "Digital Bangladesh" and the descent into authoritarian governance—are not parallel stories but are intrinsically intertwined. The very technologies heralded as instruments of liberation and development were systematically co-opted as tools of control, surveillance, and narrative manipulation. This created a "Dual State": a Janus-faced entity where one face smiles upon foreign investors and the digital-savvy middle class, while the other grimaces with repression, silencing critics and crushing opposition. The primary victims and the most potent challengers to this Dual State have been the students and youth of Bangladesh. This demographic, the largest beneficiary of the digital revolution, has also been its most prominent casualty. This paper will explore the intricate connections between state policy, technological adoption, political repression, and the resulting socio-psychological crisis. It will investigate the following central research question: How has the Sheikh Hasina government's simultaneous pursuit of technological modernization and political authoritarianism between 2014 and 2024 contributed to a cycle of student protest, public health decline, and social crime? To answer this, the thesis is structured as follows: Chapter 2 outlines the political landscape, detailing the consolidation of the Awami League's power. Chapter 3 delves into the role of AI and digital technology, with a specific case study on the "Sheikh Hasina AI Detector." Chapter 4 analyzes the key student protest movements of the decade and the state's response. Chapter 5 examines the societal fallout, focusing on mental health, suicide, and crime. Finally, Chapter 6 synthesizes these threads to present a holistic conclusion on the state of the nation. Chapter 2: The Political Landscape – The Architecture of Authoritarian Rule (2014-2024) The period from 2014 onwards represents the solidification of a competitive authoritarian regime in Bangladesh. Under competitive authoritarianism, democratic institutions formally exist but are so heavily skewed in favor of the incumbent that they fail to meet minimum democratic standards. 2.1 The 2014 and 2018 Elections: A Democratic Façade The January 2014 general election was a watershed moment. The opposition BNP, demanding a neutral caretaker government to oversee the polls—a system abolished by the Awami League in 2011—resorted to a strategy of strikes, blockades, and boycott. The result was an election where over half of the parliamentary seats were uncontested, handing the Awami League a landslide victory. The pre-election period was marked by extreme violence, including arson attacks on public transport, for which both sides blamed each other. The election fundamentally broke the conventional pattern of power alternation in Bangladesh, establishing a "winner-takes-all" system where the stakes of losing power became existential for the opposition. The 2018 election continued this trend. While the BNP participated, its leader Khaleda Zia was imprisoned on charges widely seen as politically motivated, crippling the party's campaign. On election day, widespread allegations of ballot stuffing, voter intimidation, and falsification of results were reported. The Awami League and its allies won 288 of the 300 directly elected seats. The international community, while noting the irregularities, largely accepted the outcome, prioritizing strategic and economic interests over democratic principles. This tacit acceptance further emboldened the regime. 2.2 The Legal and Extralegal Apparatus of Control The government employed a multi-pronged strategy to neutralize opposition and dissent. The Digital Security Act (DSA) 2018: This legislation became the regime's most potent legal weapon. Its vague provisions, such as criminalizing speech that "hurts religious sentiment" or "spoils communal harmony," or spreads "propaganda and campaigns against liberation war spirit," were routinely used to arrest journalists, activists, academics, and ordinary citizens for critical social media posts. The non-bailable nature of the charges created a powerful chilling effect, stifling free speech in the digital public sphere that "Digital Bangladesh" had helped create. Enforced Disappearances and Extrajudicial Killings: Human rights organizations like Human Rights Watch and Odhikar have documented hundreds of cases of enforced disappearances by state security forces, predominantly targeting opposition members, activists, and dissidents. Many victims later reappeared in state custody, charged under laws like the DSA; others were found dead, with authorities claiming they were killed in "crossfires" or "gunfights"—a euphemism for extrajudicial killing that has entered the common lexicon. Co-option of Institutions: Key state institutions, including the judiciary, the election commission, and the anti-corruption commission, were widely perceived as having lost their independence and being used to harass the opposition and protect allies of the regime. This political context is the essential backdrop against which the technological and social developments of the decade must be understood. The state's insecurity and its drive for total control fueled its interest in advanced tools of surveillance and information management. Chapter 3: The AI Revolution – Tools for Development and Instruments of Repression The "Digital Bangladesh" vision was not merely rhetorical. The government made significant investments in digital infrastructure. Mobile internet users grew from a few million to over 120 million. Services like online tax filing, utility bill payments, and university admissions became digitized, improving efficiency and reducing low-level corruption. However, this digital transformation had a dark underbelly. The same infrastructure that delivered services also enabled unprecedented state surveillance. 3.1 The Emergence of Pro-Government AI and Bot Networks By the late 2010s, a sophisticated ecosystem of pro-government propaganda emerged online. While not all of it can be definitively labeled as "AI," it demonstrated a coordinated, automated, and strategic use of digital tools. Twitter/X and Facebook Bots: During periods of political tension or student protests, social media platforms would be flooded with accounts praising the government, Sheikh Hasina, and the Awami League, while simultaneously attacking and discrediting critics, protesters, and opposition figures. These accounts often used similar language, posted in coordinated bursts, and displayed other inauthentic behavioral patterns consistent with bot networks. Astroturfing and Narrative Control: These networks were used to create a false sense of popular consensus. When a controversial policy was introduced or a protest movement gained traction, these bots would swarm hashtags and comment sections, drowning out dissenting voices and projecting an image of overwhelming public support for the government. This tactic, known as astroturfing, is a form of information warfare that manipulates public perception. Deepfakes and Synthetic Media: While less prevalent, there were instances of manipulated media being used for political purposes. This included edited videos to make protesters appear violent or to spread disinformation about opposition leaders. The potential for more advanced AI-generated deepfakes to create chaos and discredit movements represents a significant future threat. 3.2 Case Study: The "Sheikh Hasina AI Detector" Phenomenon The most potent symbol of the regime's attempt to fuse technology with political control is the "Sheikh Hasina AI Detector." This is not a single, publicly available software application, but rather a purported technological capability that has been reported in state-aligned media and hinted at by government officials. The concept revolves around using Artificial Intelligence, specifically machine learning and Natural Language Processing (NLP), to monitor the Bangladeshi digital sphere (social media, news sites, blogs) in real-time. Its stated purpose, as per pro-government narratives, is to "detect anti-state propaganda," "fake news," and "defamatory content" against the Prime Minister, the state, and the ideology of the Liberation War. Technical and Ethical Analysis: Data and Training: For such a system to work, it would require a massive dataset of online content labeled as "loyal/pro-state" and "subversive/anti-state." The fundamental problem is the political nature of these labels. A critique of a government policy on electricity tariffs could be algorithmically classified as "anti-state" if the training data is curated by a partisan entity. This creates a feedback loop where the AI learns and reinforces the ruling party's political biases. Lack of Transparency: There is no public information about the system's algorithms, its false-positive rate, or its oversight mechanisms. This opacity makes it a perfect tool for arbitrary and politically motivated enforcement. A citizen could be flagged by a black-box algorithm for a seemingly innocuous post, leading to investigation or arrest under the DSA. Function as a Chilling Effect: The mere existence of such a tool, whether fully operational or partly a myth propagated to instill fear, serves a powerful psychological purpose. It creates a panopticon effect, where citizens self-censor, believing that an all-seeing AI is constantly monitoring their digital footprints. This directly undermines the freedom of expression that a healthy digital public sphere requires. The "Sheikh Hasina AI Detector" is thus the ultimate embodiment of the Dual State. It is a technologically advanced system, born from the "Digital Bangladesh" initiative, but its primary function is authoritarian: to identify, track, and neutralize political dissent. It represents the weaponization of AI not for national security against external threats, but for regime security against its own citizens. Chapter 4: The Fire of Protest – Student Movements and State Response The political repression and the weaponization of technology did not go unchallenged. The most significant and sustained challenges came from the student community, whose protests became flashpoints that revealed the true nature of the state. 4.1 The Quota Reform Movement (2018) In April 2018, university and college students across the country launched a massive movement demanding a reform of the quota system for government jobs. The existing system reserved 56% of posts for various groups (children of freedom fighters, women, districts, etc.), which protesters argued was unfair and promoted inefficiency. The movement was largely peaceful, organized by students themselves, and cut across partisan lines. The state's response was brutal. Police and ruling party student wing, the Bangladesh Chhatra League (BCL), violently attacked protesting students on campuses and on the streets. Dozens were injured, and hundreds were arrested. The government initially agreed to abolish the quotas but later backtracked after the movement was suppressed. The key takeaway was the state's intolerance of any large-scale, non-partisan mobilization that challenged a state policy, even one led by apolitical students. 4.2 The Road Safety Protests (2018) In July 2018, the death of two students by a speeding bus in Dhaka ignited long-simmering public anger over chaotic and unregulated transport systems. For days, thousands of school and college students occupied major intersections in the capital, enforcing traffic laws themselves in a stunning display of civic responsibility. The state's response was even more severe. The BCL, alongside police and armed plainclothes individuals, launched coordinated attacks on the protesters. There were numerous reports of sexual harassment of female protesters, beatings, and kidnappings. The digital tools of the state were also deployed: false narratives portraying the students as "hooligans" and "agents of conspirators" were spread online by bot networks. The "AI Detector" logic was implicitly at play, framing legitimate civic protest as an "anti-state" activity requiring a digital and physical crackdown. 4.3 The 2024 University Dues Hike Protests A more recent example in early 2024 saw protests at public universities like the University of Dhaka against sudden and sharp hikes in tuition and other fees. Students argued this would make higher education inaccessible for the poor and middle class. Once again, the state's response was to deploy police and the BCL to violently disperse gatherings, arrest protest leaders, and shutter dormitories. Pattern of Response: A clear pattern emerges from these protests: A legitimate, grassroots grievance sparks a student-led movement. The state perceives it not as a civic issue but as a challenge to its authority. It responds with a tripartite strategy: (a) physical violence through state and party actors; (b) a disinformation campaign online to discredit the protesters; and (c) legal persecution using laws like the DSA. This cycle of protest and violent suppression has had profound psychological consequences for a generation of young Bangladeshis. Chapter 5: The Societal – and The political the crushing of by the student the of by digital surveillance, and the economic of and have created a societal crisis. The and Bangladesh has been with a of mental health and suicide, among the Data and A study by the of found that over of Bangladeshi from mental health with and being most have been rising to the Bangladesh has one of the suicide in the with a significant being young to Political and The is and For students witnessed their being and for demanding or the future can appear The of a system where is to political connections a sense of despair. in a is by the of for political expression and social civic and more Digital The of being or having a online comment by an algorithm creates a state of The digital a of and for youth, a of The state's response to this mental health crisis has been with in public mental health services and a social that from The in and The decay of social is also in rising criminality. and The same digital that "Digital Bangladesh" promoted has a dark There has been an of including and online While not directly by the state, this ecosystem in an where the state's primary digital is on political control rather citizen and The youth are increasingly to with a national crisis. The often with political and are seen as and of and violent have often such to a in social economic and the of violence by the state and its The societal is not a issue but a of the political and technological The Dual in its for control, has eroded the very social and psychological of the a generation and in to or crime. Chapter – and The decade from 2014 to 2024 under Sheikh has been