TY  - JOUR
ID  - R0236
TI  - [SGINC] CYBER SECURE E-GOVERNANCE MODELS FOR SDG DELIVERY IN DEVELOPING NATIONS: LEGAL, TECHNOLOGICAL, AND MANAGERIAL APPROACHES
AU  - Shyamashree Singha
AU  - Gouranga Bag
AU  - Samrat Kundu
AU  - Partha Pratim Dasgupta
AU  - Suparna Mahajan
PY  - 2025
JO  - Iterative International Publishers eBooks
DO  - https://doi.org/10.58532/nbennuralsnepgf11n1
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic (law, speech or online harm)] In this age of rapidly growing digital maturity, e- governance is a game-changer to drive the UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), especially the health, education, justice, and infrastructure sectors. The convergence of governance, cyber security and sustainable development is a crucial area of focus, especially in the age where governments of developing countries are relying more and more on digital platforms to provide essential public services. And, while digital change offers great potential, it also comes with substantial risks from cyber attacks, data breaches, and legal exposure, particularly in territories where regulatory and infrastructure may be weak.This paper aims to investigate also the concepts of development and assessment of cyber safe e-governance initiatives so that they can support and facilitate the delivery of the SDGs in developing nations. It focuses on the urgent challenge of embedding legal compliance, cyber security architecture and managerial oversight in national digital governance policies. Consistent with a multi disciplinary approach, it uses a qualitative analysis of legal and policy texts complemented by comparative case studies of e-governance projects in India, Kenya and Bangladesh. These countries provide meaningful case studies to explore a variety of challenges and solutions for digital governance in low-resource settings. The analysis finds that even though a number of the developing countries have achieved significant strides in the use of digital platforms for the delivery of public services— for instance, India’s Digital India initiative, Kenya’s eCitizen portal, and Bangladesh’s a2i program— there still exist critical weaknesses in terms of cybersecurity readiness, data privacy laws, and institutional capability. Principal concerns include unauthorized access, theft of identity, denial-of service, which erode public trust and may significantly disrupt SDG-aligned service delivery. In addition, lack of harmonized legal frameworks and inadequate investment in digital literacy and cybersecurity awareness further add to difficulties in digital governance. To address these issues, the study offers a multi-layered e governance model that combines secure authentication protocols, encrypted data exchanges, centralized threat monitoring, and real-time auditing tools. This all takes place within a legally compliant and ethically governed digital environment. The model highlights the need for national e-governance platforms to meet international cybersecurity standards, such as ISO/IEC 27001 and NIST, while being flexible enough to fit local socio political contexts. Additionally, strategies like capacity building, stakeholder collaboration, and ongoing risk assessment are crucial for successful implementation.The paper ends with specific recommendations for policymakers. These include creating inclusive digital laws, investing in national cybersecurity infrastructure, and promoting international partnerships to aid capacity building. By taking a secure, integrated, and context-sensitive approach to e-governance, developing countries can improve their capacity to meet the Sustainable Development Goals, build public trust in digital institutions, and ensure a more resilient and fair digital future.
