JO  - Wiley eBooks
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1002/9781119712121.ch7
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic (law, speech or online harm)] The Democratic People's Republic of Korea (DPRK) uses cyber to produce effects that span from intimidation to developing revenue for its military modernization, including nuclear development and delivery programs. Some estimates are that up to one-third of the DPRK's nuclear development and delivery program is funded through cyber theft. At the time of this writing, DPRK cyber operators are credited with the attempted $1 billion Bangladesh Bank heist (2016), the WannaCry Ransomware Attack (2017) ($4–$8 billion in damages), and ongoing digital wallet theft operations that are estimated to have garnered over $1 billion since 2017. Kim Il Sung, who founded the Democratic People's Republic of Korea (DPRK) in 1948, also formed Room 39 in the mid-1970s with his son, Kim Jong Il, to produce revenue for the family. Room 39 is a cell in the Worker's Party of Korea that includes organized crime operations, including counterfeiting, drugs, and now cyber. Kim Jong Il originally saw the benefits of using cyber for distributed communications and then for espionage and theft. Kim Jong Il's son, Kim Jong Un, ascended to power in 2011 and expanded on his father's cyber investment. This included increasing cyber operator technical training and developing computing centers in order to leverage technically adept DPRK youths. The DPRK cyber operations program now spans multiple cyber teams that include thousands of personnel with a proven ability to perform ransomware, bank, and digital wallet theft operations.
