DO  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3572334.3572396
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This note explores how various technology-mediated negative experiences and safety concerns of non-Western LGBTQ+ users, particularly from Bangladesh, hinder their continuing online interactions and self-presentation practices. Based on face-to-face and Skype semi-structured interviews (n=31), our initial results report that along with facing life-threatening harassing experiences online, Bangladeshi LGBTQ+ users also struggle with audience management and perceived privacy affordances that critically restrict their identity exploration and overall online participation, often forcing them to adopt fake/pseudo-identity online. These findings advocate for better design implications on safer social media participation, especially for LGBTQ+ users from non-Western contexts, and call for more attention to inclusive technologies.
