TI  - [SGINC] Could the Issue of DPRK Hacking Benefit from Benign Neglect?
AU  - Martin C. Libicki
PY  - 2018
JO  - Georgetown journal of international affairs
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1353/gia.2018.0010
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Could the Issue of DPRK Hacking Benefit from Benign Neglect? Martin Libicki (bio) In September 2017, in the face of fears stemming from North Korea's nuclear program, the New York Times posited that "there is another, less dramatic interpretation of Mr. Kim's intentions. The combination of his developing nuclear program and his increasingly impressive cyber program may allow the North to effectively get away with smaller provocations without fear of military retaliation. Mr. Kim paid very little price for the cyberattack that took out 70 percent of Sony Pictures Entertainment's computer systems three years ago. There was no retaliation for its attacks on South Korean banks and media companies; its suspected theft of money from the Bangladesh central bank; or its role in a recent attack that hit Britain's hospitals with ransomware demands. It might try to expand its cyberattacks for profit, or blackmail countries for economic aid."1 The article's authors probably intended to portray North Korea's bait-and-switch ploy as a bad thing. But such a proposition is hardly self-evident. If the costs of cyberspace mischief were less than the risks of today's nuclear or conventional confrontation and if resources devoted by North Korea to cyberspace mischief displaced resources going into their other threats and if different US deterrent responses to North Korea's cyberspace mischief could affect how much hacking was done by North Koreans of suppressing it, would we be better off changing our deterrence policy—and if so, in what direction? To be sure, this is a large number of successive "ifs"—all of which have to be true in the same way to conclude that US policy toward North Korea's bad behavior in cyberspace should be radically changed. So, consider each in turn. The Cost of Cyberspace Mischief Since roughly 2009 (with a coordinated set of flooding attacks against more than two dozen web sites) and increasingly thereafter, North Korea has been behaving badly in cyberspace. Initially, its efforts were aimed at South Korea. In 2011, hackers struck the Nonghyup agricultural bank, preventing its 30 million customers from using ATM services, and destroying key data.2 North Korea's Dark Seoul campaign targeted banks and media companies and destroyed the [End Page 83] functionality of tens of thousands of computers.3 Later reports suggest that North Korean hackers have penetrated nuclear power facilities4 and subway control networks in South Korea5—although, according to South Korean officials, in neither case were controls themselves jeopardized. There are also strong indications that South Korea had to redo its national identification system, at the cost of almost a billion dollars, as a result of North Korean depredations.6 Setting aside whether it was North Korea that carried out these attacks (probably, although such matters are rarely open-and-shut cases), even estimating the costs of such incidents to South Korea is a tricky business. One must wrestle with imponderables such as what customers actually did in lieu of using inoperative ATM machines. Furthermore, determining the importance of squelching North Korea's cyberspace threat requires some guesswork as to whether what North Korea did represents the limit of its abilities or, instead, self-restraint. But why should North Korea hold itself back? After all, North Korea has few beneficial ties to South Korea that could be jeopardized by intensifying its efforts, and there is scant indication of a narrative that suggests it is holding back its cyber-attacks as a quid pro quo for South Korea's forbearance. A country that in 1987 was willing to blow up an airliner using a bomb might not be inhibited from taking one down using malware. By contrast, it gives every indication that it could wreak far more physical violence than it does against South Korea if so motivated. Correspondingly, an assessment that an all-out North Korean assault in cyberspace could wreak a billion dollars a year in damage is not wildly implausible.7 Unfortunately, North Korea's hacking has not been limited to its peninsula. The 2014 attack on Sony is a case in point; according to Sony's filings with the SEC, it lost $35 million8...
