AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Mobile banking has revolutionized financial inclusion by facilitating mobile-based banking transactions via mobile phones, mobile apps, digital wallets, UPI platforms and other electronic means of payment.But this digital banking platform that enables financial inclusion also presents a significant channel for white collar cyber crimes.This article offers a legal research perspective on mobile banking and suggests that mobile banking serves as a digital-financial gateway that raises complex issues of consent, authentication, consumer protection, banking liability, data protection, criminal fraud, money laundering and international enforcement.The article reviews Indian policy documents, police warnings, bank cybersecurity advisories, academic articles and a few national and global case studies to explore phishing, vishing, smishing, SIM swap, mobile malware, fraudulent banking apps, account takeover, mule accounts and cryptocurrency-related frauds.It also assesses the cases of State Bank of India v. Pallabh Bhowmik & Ors., Hare Ram Singh v. Reserve Bank of India & Ors., Subodh C. Korde v. Union of India & Ors., Jaiprakash Kulkarni & Anr.v. Banking Ombudsman & Ors., Bangladesh Bank cyber heist, Operation Mule Hunt 2.0 and the Anatsa Android banking trojan attack.The article argues that mobile banking is not the direct source of white collar cyber crime, but has become a crime-enabling platform due to its real-time, remote processing, device dependency, instant payment systems, OTP authentication and institutional segregation.
