JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21182445
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid expansion of digital technology in India has transformed communication, banking, education, and commerce, but it has also led to a significant rise in cybercrimes. Offences such as online fraud, phishing, identity theft, hacking, cyberstalking, QR code scams, and digital arrest scams have become increasingly common, affecting individuals across all age groups. The growing dependence on digital platforms, combined with a lack of cybersecurity awareness, has made internet users more vulnerable to cybercriminals. To combat these challenges, India has established a legal framework through the Information Technology Act, 2000, which provides penalties for offences such as identity theft, online impersonation, and the publication of obscene content. Victims may also seek remedies under other criminal laws and can report incidents through the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal or the national cyber helpline (1930). Additionally, cybercrime police stations and cyber cells play an important role in investigating online offences and assisting victims. Despite these legal safeguards, prevention remains the most effective defence against cybercrime. Simple practices such as protecting passwords and OTPs, enabling two-factor authentication, avoiding suspicious links, and reporting fraudulent activities promptly can significantly reduce risks. As India continues its digital transformation, strengthening cybersecurity awareness, promoting responsible online behaviour, and ensuring effective legal enforcement are essential to creating a safe and secure digital environment for all citizens.
N1  - R0584 | tier B
