AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid growth of digital technology has increased the risk of Cyber Crimes, making effective legal remedies essential in India. This article examines the major forms of Cyber Crimes, including hacking, identity theft, online financial fraud, cyberstalking, data breaches and phishing. It analyses the legal framework addressing these offences, particularly the Information Technology Act, 2000, the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, and the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023. The study also highlights the importance of electronic evidence, data protection, cybersecurity and constitutional rights, including privacy and freedom of speech. It discusses the remedies available to victims, such as reporting offences, initiating criminal proceedings, seeking compensation and requesting the removal of unlawful online content. Furthermore, it identifies challenges in cybercrime investigation, jurisdiction, enforcement and emerging technologies. The article concludes that effective cybercrime prevention requires stronger law enforcement, digital literacy, technological expertise, institutional cooperation and continuous legal reforms to ensure a secure and rights-respecting digital environment.
