AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Digital systems are both targets of criminal attacks and instruments through which familiar offences are committed. Indian law responds through a combination of the Information Technology Act, 2000, the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 and rules governing electronic evidence. This article examines how those frameworks allocate criminal responsibility for unauthorised access, identity misuse, online deception, digital forgery and related conduct. Using doctrinal analysis, it distinguishes proof that an incident occurred from proof that an accused committed the relevant offence with the required mental element. It also evaluates intermediary and corporate liability, the treatment of electronic records and the practical limits of cross-border enforcement. The article argues that broader criminalisation alone cannot resolve difficulties of attribution and proof. Effective enforcement requires reliable preservation of digital material, offence-specific investigation, trained institutions and constitutional safeguards. A coordinated approach should protect victims and digital infrastructure while avoiding liability based merely on account ownership, corporate designation or unpopular online expression.
