AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid expansion of digital technologies has transformed the social, economic and cultural lives of young people in India. Smart phones, social networking platforms, online gaming, digital payments, e-commerce and virtual communication have become integral components of contemporary youth culture. At the same time, increasing digital dependence has created new forms of vulnerability and criminality. Young people may experience cybercrime as victims, witnesses, offenders, or intermediaries in digital criminal networks. Cyber bullying, identity theft, online financial fraud, phishing, social-media harassment, sextortion, impersonation, hacking, digital blackmail and the circulation of harmful content represent some of the emerging challenges associated with the digital environment. Uttar Pradesh, as India’s most populous state and one with extensive digital expansion, faces a particularly complex cybercrime-management environment. The Uttar Pradesh Police has developed specialized cybercrime institutions and technological mechanisms in response to the changing nature of crime. The state police identify a Cyber Crime Headquarters at Lucknow and have established dedicated cybercrime policing structures; its official material states that the state conceptualized an exclusive cybercrime police station in 2016 and subsequently facilitated cyber police stations across its 18 police ranges. The National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal and the 1930 cybercrime helpline have further strengthened mechanisms for reporting cyber offenses and financial fraud. This research paper examines the relationship between youth, cybercrime and digital policing with particular reference to Uttar Pradesh Police. It explores the changing nature of youth vulnerability in cyberspace, emerging forms of cybercrime, institutional responses, digital investigation, cyber awareness and the potential contribution of artificial intelligence and data-driven policing. The study employs a qualitative, descriptive and analytical methodology based primarily on secondary sources, including government reports, official police documents, legal materials, academic literature and institutional publications. The findings indicate that technological policing can improve the speed of reporting, investigation, evidence management and prevention; however, technological capacity alone cannot address the social dimensions of cybercrime. Lack of cyber awareness, under-reporting, digital inequality, shortage of specialized personnel, privacy concerns, algorithmic bias and rapidly changing criminal techniques remain significant challenges. The paper argues for a youth-centered model of digital policing that combines technological modernization with cyber education, community policing, ethical safeguards, institutional training and collaboration among police, educational institutions, families, technology companies and civil society.
