AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid development of artificial intelligence has completely transformed the digital world, bringing both benefits and risks. Malicious actors are increasingly using artificial intelligence systems to commit cybercrimes, giving rise to new cybercrimes as these systems are integrated into communications, economics, healthcare, and governance. While it strengthens cyber security, it also poses a threat to traditional cyber law through artificial intelligence-enabled cybercrime, ranging from automated phishing attacks and artificial intelligence-enabled deep fakes to autonomous malware. By critically engaging with the ethical, procedural, and regulatory challenges posed by cybercrimes perpetrated by artificial intelligence, this research seeks to contribute to a forward-thinking legal framework that balances the accountability of innovation and digital growth with human rights. India has established a regulatory framework for cybercrime through the provisions of the IT Act 2000 and the Indian Penal Code 2023. This research examines in depth the challenges posed by cybercrimes perpetrated by artificial intelligence in India. The paper also suggests legal and policy reforms that can strengthen India's ability to address emerging cyber threats in the era of artificial intelligence.
