AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Sexually explicit deepfakes have moved, within a few years, from a curiosity of machine-learning forums to an ordinary instrument of harassment, extortion and humiliation. Most of those targeted are women, and in India the injury is magnified by the social weight attached to sexual reputation. This paper examines how Indian criminal law and procedure have absorbed the phenomenon. It argues that the substantive law, even after the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, still reads synthetic sexual imagery through the older vocabularies of obscenity and a woman’s modesty, neither of which captures the wrong of fabricating a person’s sexual self without consent. Regulatory energy has gone instead into intermediary obligations, culminating in the Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Amendment Rules, 2026. Takedown matters to victims, but administrative speed cannot substitute for a coherent offence or for a criminal process that does not reinjure those who use it. Drawing on judicial developments in Delhi and Madras, comparative legislation and the literature on image-based sexual abuse, the paper proposes a narrowly drawn, gender-neutral offence centred on consent, together with procedural protections for complainants, while taking seriously the objections grounded in free expression and in the critique of carceral responses.
