AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] ABSTRACT: The exponential growth of digital infrastructure in India has been accompanied by a corresponding surge in cyber crime, presenting unprecedented challenges to legal frameworks, law enforcement institutions, and criminological understanding. This review paper synthesizes existing literature on cyber crime in India with particular focus on Madhya Pradesh, examining the legal architecture, institutional responses, and emerging technological challenges. Drawing on Routine Activity Theory and its adaptations to cyber crime contexts, the review analyzes the evolution of the Information Technology Act, 2000, its amendments, and judicial interpretations. Key findings reveal significant gaps between statutory frameworks and operational realities: overlapping provisions between the IT Act and the Indian Penal Code create interpretive complexities; institutional capacity remains severely constrained; conviction rates remain alarmingly low; and emerging threats such as AI-enabled fraud present unprecedented challenges. The paper identifies critical areas for reform, including legislative consolidation, specialized judicial infrastructure, enhanced training programs, and improved inter-agency coordination. The review contributes to criminological scholarship on cyber crime in developing economy contexts and provides a foundation for future empirical research. Keywords: cyber crime, Routine Activity Theory, Information Technology Act, institutional capacity, Madhya Pradesh, cyber stalking, digital forensics, AI-enabled crime
