AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The fast development of Artificial Intelligence (AI) has reshaped the digital environment, providing numerous possibilities to innovate as it has also made possible advanced types of cybercrime. Women are now among the most susceptible people to the AI-enhanced cyber crime, such as deepfake pornography, cyberstalking, identity theft, voice cloning, online harassment, sextortion, and online impersonation. These crimes are against the right of women to privacy, dignity, reputation and autonomy and lead to extreme psychological, social and economic impacts. By critically analyzing the novelty of AI-led cyber crimes committed against women and considering the sufficiency of an existing legal system in India, this paper assesses these issues. It compares the protection rights of Articles 14, 15, 19, and 21 in the Constitution of India, the provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, the Information Technology Act, 2000, and the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023. Additional judicial rulings, uncovered gaps in the legal framework, and obstacles like the lack of AI-specific laws, issues in assigning criminal liability, cross-border regulation, and digital forensics are also discussed in the paper. It also ends by providing a set of recommendations to protect women within the emerging AI-driven digital world including extensive legal changes, strengthening institutional capacity, platform responsibility, digital literacy, and inter-country collaboration.
