AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Abstract Backed by the Unified Payments Interface (UPI) platform, India's rapid digital financial infrastructure development has been accompanied by a corresponding proliferation of cyber-enabled financial fraud, linking to the core issue of the economics of digital trust. This paper looks at the entire Indian financial sector cybercrime regulatory architecture from a law and economics perspective that conceptualizes digital trust as a public good under attack by information asymmetries, negative externalities, and a regulator hypothesized to be incapable of effective enforcement. Employing official, primary data on India‘s financial cybercrime regulatory agencies the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C), and the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal (NCRP) over the study period of rapid proliferation of digital transactions and modest strengthening of the security infrastructure around it, this paper tracks the regulatory/transnational frontier‘s ability to keep pace with (i.e., outgrow) the exponential proliferation of digital transactions with limited, compound-annual-growth-rate-comparison (CAGR) data versus an over-time descriptive impression. It evaluates recent reforms, including the RBI's 2026 consolidated Cybersecurity, Technology, Risk, Resilience and Assurance Directions, against classical law-and-economics frameworks of deterrence, transaction costs, and regulatory capture. It finds that, from primary regulatory sources, India’s financial cybercrime architecture remained institutionally fragmented across the RBI, the IT Ministry’s CERT-In, and the Home Ministry‘s I4C ecosystem in general until an RBI consolidation of 628 circulars rolled out a unified 64 directions in 2026. In addition, the paper finds confirmed, verified compound-annual-growth-rate-outpace growth of fraud-loss outpaces growth of digital transactions, but the downstream economic impact of this institutional fragmentation on enforcement and indirect sanction point costs appears to be an empirical issue for future study. It ends with notes on a catch-up model for digitalization-supported financial services. Keywords: Cybercrime; Digital Trust; Law and Economics; Financial Regulation; Digital Payments
N1  - R0646 | tier B
