TI  - [SGINC] Cyber Fraud and Identity Theft: Legal Challenges and Remedies in India
AU  - Satya Prakash Pandey
AU  - Prof. (Dr.) Rakesh Kumar
PY  - 2026
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22894457
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Cyber fraud and identity theft expose a discontinuity between the criminalisation of digital misconduct and the restoration of the person harmed. This article examines whether Indian law connects offender liability, financial loss allocation and identity repair within a coherent remedial framework. Employing doctrinal analysis of legislation, judicial decisions and regulatory instruments, it distinguishes dishonest appropriation of identifiers from electronic personation and from transactions induced by deception. It argues that the principal difficulty is not an absence of offences but the fragmentation of duties among investigating agencies, financial institutions and data custodians. Particular attention is given to the interaction between the Information Technology Act 2000 and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023, judicially supervised recovery under the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita 2023, and the temporal limits of banking and data-protection reforms. The article develops a differentiated remedial model comprising immediate preservation, reasoned allocation of financial responsibility, and correction of compromised identity records. Its central conclusion is that effective protection requires institutional coordination without conflating authentication with consent, regulatory penalties with compensation, or suspicion with criminal culpability.
