ID  - R0648
TI  - [SGINC] CYBERSTALKING AND ONLINE HARASSMENT IN INDIA: MULTI-STAKEHOLDER EVIDENCE ON VICTIMISATION, REPORTING, LEGAL ADEQUACY AND ENFORCEMENT CAPACITY IN HARYANA
AU  - Sawan Ahlawat
AU  - Anamika Yadav
PY  - 2026
JO  - ShodhKosh Journal of Visual and Performing Arts
DO  - https://doi.org/10.29121/shodhkosh.v7.i1.2026.9356
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Cyberstalking and online harassment have grown rapidly in India, yet little primary evidence compares what victims, investigators, lawyers and policymakers actually experience and believe. This paper reports a four-group survey of 400 respondents in Gurugram, Rohtak, Faridabad and Panchkula (Haryana): 200 victims, 80 law-enforcement officials, 70 legal practitioners and experts, and 50 policymakers and information-technology (IT) professionals. Structured questionnaires were analysed with frequencies, one-way ANOVA, the Kruskal–Wallis test, one-sample t-tests, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, binomial tests and chi-square tests. Victims were mostly women (70.0%) aged 18–35 years (76.5%); repeated unwanted messages or calls (55.5%) and harassment through WhatsApp (87.0%) were the most common experiences. Only 33.5% of victims reported the incident, and 59.7% of those who did were dissatisfied with the response; 72.0% were not fully aware that cyberstalking is punishable. Among legal experts, 78.9% of those who took a position rated the framework inadequate, only 12.9% considered remedies accessible and 81.4% favoured more specific statutory provisions. Only 18.8% of police respondents reported adequate forensic tools. By contrast, all 55 agreement-scale statements produced mean scores close to the neutral value (2.88–3.08), with no significant differences between groups (ANOVA p = 0.997). The two hypotheses, on legal inadequacy and on the hindering effect of limited awareness and expertise, are therefore partially supported: by the categorical indicators but not by the opinion scales. The findings point to under-reporting, evidentiary difficulty and forensic capacity as the central practical problems.
