TI  - [SGINC] Cyber Fraud and Digital Financial Crimes in India: Emerging Criminal-Law Challenges
AU  - Md. Tahir Hasan
AU  - Asra Fathima Syed
PY  - 2026
JO  - International Journal of Law Management & Humanities
DO  - https://doi.org/10.63108/ijlmh.13055
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] With the rapid transformation of the financial landscape in India, digital payment systems, internet banking services, mobile applications and financial technology have changed the way financial transactions are carried out. They have also opened new avenues of financial fraud for cybercriminals, who use techniques such as phishing, identity theft, social engineering, unauthorised electronic transactions, account takeover, investment scams and SIM-swap fraud. The Reserve Bank of India’s Annual Report 2024–25 records 13,516 card/internet frauds reported by banks during the year, involving ₹520 crore, which highlights the continuing importance of digital financial fraud in the banking sector. The paper examines whether the current criminal-law structure in India is sufficient to tackle the new forms of cyber fraud. It examines the Information Technology Act 2000, the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023, the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam 2023 and the regulatory framework of the Reserve Bank of India. It also explores issues of attribution, mens rea, electronic evidence and cross-border investigation, as well as the restitution of money and cooperation between law enforcement and the financial sector. The real problem is not that there are no criminal provisions at all, but that traditional criminal law is applied in a piecemeal fashion to technologically advanced conduct. The paper suggests enhanced digital-forensic capability, more rapid financial-fraud response processes, stronger evidentiary processes, better institutional accountability and greater use of technology in investigation and prevention.
