AU  - Rao Qasim Idrees
AU  - Naveed Hussain
AU  - Ali Shahid
PY  - 2026
JO  - Journal for Current Sign
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Digital technologies, internet penetration, and internet-based platform have swept Pakistan and revolutionized the process of socialization, economic and governmental affairs, and created novel forms of crime, in the cyber space. Among the newest and earliest ways to deal with these emerging threats has been the passing of cybercrime laws in Pakistan this year in the shape of the Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act, 2016 (PECA), the first full body law of cybercrime in Pakistan. The article appears to be a critical discourse on PECA 2016 in the context of substantive law, in practice, and the overall implication to primary rights, government, and legal certainty. It claims that PECA is applicable to the legislative gap since the Act criminalizes a broad scope of cyber crimes, including unauthorized access, cyber terrorism, electronic fraud, and Internet-based harassment, though the Act has conceptual flaws, over-definition, and enforcement based provisions, which create the problem of due process, freedom of expression, privacy, and proportionality becoming earnest consideration. The article also argues that the ineffective implementation of the law by the executive agencies through weak institutional capacity, lack of technical knowledge and concentration of investigative powers will hamper the implementation of the law in a manner that is both effective and respectful of rights. This paper points to the conflict between cybersecurity and civil rights by placing PECA in the context of constitutionally protected provisions of Pakistan and the international frameworks of cybercrime and human rights. It finds that the current state of PECA, devoid of substantive legal reform, articulated protection, and additional control mechanisms, puts the tool under assessment as a means of control rather than a balanced means of cybercrime prevention and digital justice.
