TI  - [SGINC] The Dark Side of Artificial Intelligence: Emerging Financial Cybercrimes, Predictive Policing and Challenges of Criminal Liability under Indian Law
AU  - B SUMIBALA
PY  - 2026
JO  - INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF CREATIVE RESEARCH THOUGHTS
DO  - https://doi.org/10.56975/ijcrt.v14i10.401646
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid expansion of digital financial services, online banking facilities, and electronic payment systems has significantly transformed financial transactions in India while simultaneously contributing to the increasing incidence of financial cyber fraud. Cyber offences, including digital arrest scams, phishing, vishing, identity theft, online banking fraud, credit card fraud, cryptocurrency-related scams, and digital payment fraud, have emerged as serious challenges to the effective administration of the criminal justice system. In this evolving technological landscape, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has assumed considerable significance as an advanced technological tool for strengthening cybercrime detection, monitoring, investigation, and prevention through predictive analytics, machine learning, and data-driven policing mechanisms. Predictive policing, which utilises AI-based algorithms and computational techniques to identify crime patterns, assess potential risks, and anticipate criminal activities, has increasingly emerged as an important strategy for addressing the growing complexities of financial cybercrime. Against this background, the present article critically examines the role and effectiveness of Artificial Intelligence and predictive policing in combating financial cyber fraud within the Indian criminal justice framework. It analyses the adequacy of the Information Technology Act, 2000, the relevant provisions of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, and the regulatory directions and guidelines issued by the Reserve Bank of India in addressing emerging forms of technology-enabled financial offences. Further, the study evaluates the application of AI-powered fraud detection mechanisms, behavioural pattern analysis, facial recognition systems, and automated risk-assessment technologies in contemporary cybercrime prevention and investigation. Simultaneously, it investigates the legal, constitutional, and ethical challenges associated with AI-assisted predictive policing, particularly concerning the right to privacy, algorithmic discrimination, excessive surveillance, lack of transparency, accountability, and the protection of fundamental rights. Adopting a doctrinal and analytical research methodology, the article assesses whether the existing Indian legal and regulatory framework provides adequate safeguards for the deployment of Artificial Intelligence in policing while maintaining the principles of fairness, proportionality, transparency, and constitutional accountability. The study ultimately proposes appropriate legislative reforms, institutional mechanisms, and regulatory safeguards to ensure the responsible and effective utilisation of Artificial Intelligence in preventing financial cyber fraud and strengthening the administration of criminal justice in India.
