AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid growth of digital technologies and internet penetration in India has transformed communication, governance, and socioeconomic participation. Women, in particular, have benefited from enhanced access to digital platforms that enable education, employment, and social engagement. However, the digital revolution has exposed women to new and emerging forms of cybercrime. Online harassment, cyberstalking, trolling, identity theft, morphing, deepfake exploitation, and Online harassment, cyberstalking, trolling, identity theft, morphing, deepfake exploitation, and unauthorised sharing of intimate images have all become common forms of digital violence. This research paper adopts a doctrinal and socio-legal approach to investigating cybercrime against women in India. It examines constitutional safeguards, statutory provisions, and judicial responses, as well as statistics from the National Crime Records Bureau. The study identifies key challenges in legal enforcement and socio-cultural barriers, and it aims to provide a comprehensive framework for preventing cybercrime against women.
