TI  - [SGINC] Deepfakes and Cyber Law: Is India's Legal Framework Ready?
AU  - Bhumi pal
PY  - 2026
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24220009
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This article examines whether India's legal framework has kept pace with the rapid growth of deepfake technology. It surveys the pre-existing toolkit under the Information Technology Act, 2000 (Sections 66C, 66D, 66E, 67, 67A) and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, and shows how each provision applies only awkwardly to deepfakes, since none was drafted with synthetic media in mind. It then examines the 2026 Amendment to the Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code Rules, which for the first time defines "synthetically generated information," mandates labelling and embedded metadata, and compresses takedown timelines to three hours for content flagged by a court or government order. The article situates these obligations alongside the Delhi High Court's personality-rights jurisprudence (Amitabh Bachchan v. Rajat Nagi, Anil Kapoor v. Simply Life India) and the constitutional right to privacy recognised in Puttaswamy, while testing the new takedown regime against the free-speech safeguards affirmed in Shreya Singhal. It identifies persisting gaps — anonymous and cross-border creators, metadata stripped on re-sharing, imperfect detection tools, and the absence of a standalone statutory right over one's likeness — and argues that India, while now possessing one of the more detailed regulatory frameworks for synthetic media, must move beyond platform-facing obligations toward enforceable individual rights, faster victim-facing remedies, and public media literacy.
