AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This paper focuses on analyzing determinants of cybercrime preparedness using a panel data econometrics approach among 48 countries based in the MENA and South Asia regions from 2018 to 2022. By using data available in the World Development Index hosted at the World Bank site, this research utilizes a panel analysis with Driscoll and Kraay standard errors to correct for cross-section dependence. The study shows that when countries are more digitally penetrative and when better governance standards for fighting corruption and rule of law are achieved, there are substantial gains toward safeguarding against cybercrime attacks. However, this study finds no evidence for literacy as a factor for preparedness against cybercrime. This clearly indicates that a broader conceptualization of cybersecurity covers more than purely technical parameters and must therefore include governance factors especially those that pertain to public integrity.
