AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Deepfakes are AI-generated synthetic media that convincingly depict a person saying or doing things they never did. Though they have lawful uses in entertainment, education and accessibility, their misuse for impersonation, fraud, harassment, non-consensual sexual imagery and disinformation threatens autonomy, privacy, consent and reputation. This article examines how deepfakes are created, the harms they cause and the Indian legal framework that applies to them: Article 21 of the Constitution, the Information Technology Act, 2000, the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, and the IT Rules, 2021, as amended in February 2026 to cover synthetically generated information. It argues that these laws punish the consequences of deepfakes but do not regulate deepfakes themselves. It proposes stricter penalties, quick and affordable remedies, stronger personality and likeness rights, careful scrutiny of digital evidence and greater public awareness, so that individual rights are protected without stifling legitimate uses of AI.
