AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The exponential proliferation of internet connectivity and digital technologies has engendered a complex and rapidly evolving landscape of criminal activity that transcends conventional geographical and jurisdictional boundaries. Cybercrime, encompassing offences ranging from hacking and data breaches to cyberterrorism and online financial fraud, poses unprecedented challenges to existing criminal law frameworks that were designed for a pre-digital era. This paper undertakes a comprehensive analytical examination of cybercrime through the lens of criminal law, focusing on the jurisprudential, legislative, and enforcement dimensions of the digital battlefield. Drawing upon Indian statutory frameworks principally the Information Technology Act, 2000 (as amended in 2008) and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 [37] alongside comparative analysis of international instruments such as the Budapest Convention on Cybercrime, 2001, and legislative models from the United States and European Union, this study critically evaluates the adequacy of existing legal responses. The paper further explores doctrinal tensions including jurisdictional conflicts, evidentiary challenges posed by digital forensics, the attribution problem inherent to anonymous cyber actors, and the emerging threat paradigm of artificial intelligence-enabled cybercrime. The authors argue that effective criminal law responses to cybercrime necessitate not merely legislative amendments but a fundamental reconceptualisation of criminal liability, jurisdictional doctrine, and international cooperation frameworks. Recommendations for law reform and policy innovation are advanced, with particular attention to the Indian context.
