JO  - International Journal of Law Management & Humanities
DO  - https://doi.org/10.63108/ijlmh.13067
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Artificial intelligence has transformed the creation, manipulation and interpretation of digital information. Deepfakes and other forms of AI-generated content can reproduce human faces, voices, movements and conversations with a degree of realism that makes ordinary visual or auditory inspection unreliable. These developments raise important questions for criminal justice, particularly where video recordings, audio clips, photographs, social-media content and digitally generated material are relied upon to establish criminal liability. In India, the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 (BSA), provides the principal statutory framework governing electronic and digital records, but compliance with the rules of admissibility does not necessarily establish the factual truth of the content presented. This paper critically examines the challenges posed by deepfakes and AI-generated evidence in Indian criminal trials. It analyses the distinction between admissibility, authenticity, attribution and evidentiary weight, with particular attention to Sections 39 and 61–63 of the BSA, the Information Technology Act, 2000, and the constitutional guarantees of equality, personal liberty and fair trial. It also considers the continuing relevance of judicial decisions concerning electronic evidence under the former Indian Evidence Act, 1872. The research adopts a doctrinal and analytical methodology based on statutory provisions, judicial precedents and relevant legal scholarship. It argues that electronic-record certification, hash values and conventional chain-of-custody procedures are important safeguards but cannot independently establish that a recording depicts a genuine event or identifies the correct person. The paper recommends risk-sensitive forensic examination, transparent disclosure of relevant technical findings, effective opportunities for cross-examination, and judicial caution against treating digital recordings as inherently reliable. A balanced framework should protect the integrity of criminal proceedings without allowing unsubstantiated allegations of AI manipulation to undermine genuine evidence.
