AU  - Vishva Nath Sharma
PY  - 2026
JO  - Research Vidyapith International Multidisciplinary Journal.
DO  - https://doi.org/10.70650/rvimj.2026v3i7006
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Online financial fraud has emerged as one of the most serious cybercrime challenges in India due to the rapid expansion of digital banking, online payments, mobile financial services, and internet-based transactions. This paper examines the legal framework, investigative mechanisms, institutional responsibilities, and victim-remedy systems governing online financial fraud in India. It analyses the role of the Information Technology Act, 2000, the Indian Penal Code, 1860, financial regulations, and other economic laws in addressing offences such as phishing, identity theft, payment fraud, data theft, malware attacks, and cyber-enabled money laundering. The study also highlights major procedural difficulties, including collection and preservation of electronic evidence, maintenance of chain of custody, tracing of digital money trails, jurisdictional conflicts, cross-border investigations, and limited forensic capacity. It evaluates the functions of police agencies, cybercrime cells, financial regulators, and digital forensic institutions in investigation and enforcement. In addition, the paper discusses civil, administrative, compensatory, and restitutionary remedies available to victims. Comparative perspectives are used to identify international best practices. The study concludes that stronger legal definitions, specialized investigation mechanisms, improved inter-agency coordination, enhanced digital forensic infrastructure, public awareness, and effective victim-recovery systems are essential for combating online financial fraud in India.
