TI  - [SGINC] Cybercrimes against women in India and Pakistan: An overview from digital abuse to accountability
AU  - Umeed-e-Sahar
PY  - 2026
JO  - VTechWorks (Virginia Tech)
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This report examines how India and Pakistan respond to cybercrime against women and asks whether women who report online abuse obtain justice and protection. Drawing on official statistics from India’s National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal and National Crime Records Bureau, parliamentary committee reports, Ministry of Home Affairs data, Pakistani government figures, and civil society research, it traces abuse from the forms it takes through reporting, institutional response and prosecution. The findings point to an accountability gap at different stages in each country. In India, only 2.4% of the 5.39 million complaints filed between 2019 and 2024 became First Information Reports, reported cases against women more than doubled between 2021 and 2025, and court pendency is high. In Pakistan, the 2025 data record 31 convictions against 142,272 complaints. The report finds that the gap is driven by limited technical and forensic capacity, slow platform cooperation, fragmented coordination, and social stigma. Outspoken women, journalists and religious and ethnic minorities are the most frequent targets, and generative AI and platform-based abuse are intensifying the harm. Rather than new laws or portals, the report recommends strengthening existing institutions through training, case prioritization, faster content removal, transparent case data, and victim-centered support. It argues that progress should be measured by the number of women able to seek justice, not by the number of laws enacted or complaints filed.
