JO  - Pakistan Journal of Psychological Research
DO  - https://doi.org/10.33824/pjpr.2026.41.3.24
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Problematic online gaming has become an increasing concern among students in recent years.The present study sought to investigate whether cyberbullying perpetration mediates the relationship between problematic online gaming and aggression.Using a cross-sectional correlational design, data were collected through purposive sampling technique from 310 university students aged 18 to 35 years (M = 23.10,SD = 2.89).The study utilized three questionnaires, namely Gaming Addiction Scale for Adolescents (Lemmens et al., 2009), Buss Perry Aggression Questionnaire (Buss & Perry, 1992), and the Cyberbullying Perpetration Subscale of the Cyberbullying Perpetration and Victimization Questionnaire (Lee et al., 2017).Correlational findings revealed that problematic online gaming was positively correlated with cyberbullying perpetration and aggression.Additionally, cyberbullying perpetration was positively correlated with aggression.Further analysis demonstrated that cyberbullying perpetration significantly mediated the relationship between problematic online gaming and aggression.These findings highlight the indirect pathway from problematic online gaming to aggression through cyberbullying perpetration, while the direct effect remained nonsignificant.
