TI  - [SGINC] Jurisdictional Dilemmas of Digital Arrest in Cross Border Cybercrime in India
AU  - Aayush  Verma
AU  - Shrikant Ambdkar
AU  - Bhavya Mittal
PY  - 2026
JO  - International Journal of Legal Science and Innovation
DO  - https://doi.org/10.63108/ijlsi.7024
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] “Digital arrest” is a form of cyber-enabled fraud in which offenders impersonate police officers, investigators, regulators, customs officials, or judicial authorities and create the false appearance that a victim is under criminal investigation or custody. Victims may be kept on video or telephone calls for hours and pressured to transfer money to accounts controlled directly or indirectly by the offenders. The distinctive legal difficulty is geographical. The caller may be in another State or another country; the victim may be in Lucknow or another Indian city; the first recipient account may be located in a different State; later transactions may cross several jurisdictions; and the digital infrastructure used for communication may be situated abroad. This article examines whether Indian criminal law can determine the appropriate forum for such a distributed offence. It argues that Indian law possesses substantial domestic jurisdictional capacity: the place where electronic communication is received, the place where the consequence occurs, and the place where property is delivered can connect the offence to an Indian court. The principal domestic difficulty is the multiplicity of potentially competent forums. The cross-border problem is different: Indian law may prescribe jurisdiction over foreign conduct, yet foreign evidence, platforms, and accused persons remain beyond direct coercive reach. The article proposes a “Digital Arrest Jurisdiction Nexus” based on five factors (act, communication, victim, consequence, and evidence) and a six-stage procedure for investigators and courts.
