AU  - Loveleen Gaur
AU  - Praveen Tomar
PY  - 2026
JO  - CRC Press eBooks
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003784876-11
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This chapter provides a critical overview of the evolving legal, ethical, and regulatory frameworks of deepfake technology and how these technologies have created a new “quasi-legal” environment. It focuses on three different jurisdictions (the United States, the European Union, and India) to compare the respective approaches taken by each. The European Union has taken the most definitive stance on deepfake regulation by establishing a specific requirement for every deepfake video to contain an explicit indication that the video is a deepfake under Article 50 of the EU Artificial Intelligence Regulation (2024/1689). Conversely, the US has taken a piecemeal approach to regulating deepfake technology, only criminalizing the use of nonconsensual visual representation of intimate activity through the enactment of the 2025 TAKE IT DOWN Act, with no other types of harm addressed under federal law, but rather relying predominantely on state law to regulate. In India, deepfakes are regulated under three different statutes, namely, the Information Technology Act 2000, the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023, and the recent amendments to the Information Technology Rules. It discusses topics related to the detection of deepfakes, policies, remedies available to victims of deepfakes, the admissibility of deepfakes in court hearings, cross-border enforcement of deepfake violations, and the ethical considerations surrounding the entire discussion. The conclusion will address the manner in which existing deepfake regulatory gaps can be closed while still preserving and supporting legitimate uses of deepfake technology.
N1  - R0759 | tier B
