TY  - JOUR
ID  - R0776
TI  - [SGINC] Synthetic Truth: A qualitative analysis of node between AI Deepfakes and Sri Lankan government regulations
AU  - Halhinna Guruge Chamari Priyadarshani
PY  - 2026
JO  - KTH Publication Database DiVA (KTH Royal Institute of Technology)
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This study examines how Sri Lanka’s existing regulatory framework addresses Artificial Intelligence deepfake related harms, evaluates the conditions affecting its effectiveness and enforceability, and considers appropriate reforms that balance regulatory effectiveness, rights protection and institutional feasibility. The research adopts a qualitative doctrinal legal methodology supported by structured documentary analysis and Braun and Clarke’s Reflexive Thematic Analysis. Relevant Sri Lankan legislation, regulatory instruments and policy materials were examined through provision-led inductive coding and cross-document comparison. Floridi’s Infosphere Theory was applied only after the thematic findings had been validated, providing an interpretive lens through which the implications for informational authenticity, identity and governance were assessed. The analysis identifies five interconnected findings. Sri Lankan law provides partial protection for informational authenticity and personal representation through computer crime, data protection, online safety and electronic evidence mechanisms. However, this protection remains fragmented across separate statutory categories. Regulatory responsibility is distributed among multiple institutions, while enforceability is affected by evidential requirements, jurisdictional constraints, procedural overlap and the operational status of relevant legal provisions. Although technology neutral rules permit some adaptation to synthetic media, the existing framework does not provide a unified approach to provenance, synthetic identity, authentication, institutional coordination and redress. The study concludes that Sri Lanka does not face complete legal absence, but rather conditional legal capability within a fragmented regulatory structure. It recommends targeted, coordinated and staged reforms, including clearer synthetic media definitions, proportionate disclosure and provenance measures, accessible remedies, stronger institutional coordination mechanisms and cross-border cooperation.
