TI  - [SGINC] The Deepfake Threat in the Global South Institutional Vulnerability and Justice System Capacity in Pakistan
AU  - Akbar ali Datoo
AU  - Muhammad Owais Rabbani
AU  - Alishah Aziz Gulzar
AU  - Mohammad Abdullah Hamid Gul
PY  - 2026
JO  - Research Journal for Social Affairs
DO  - https://doi.org/10.71317/rjsa.004.01.1126
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] While scholarly attention to AI-generated deepfakes has focused predominantly on technologically advanced Western jurisdictions, the unique vulnerabilities of developing countries remain critically underexplored. This article addresses this gap by examining the deepfake threat in Pakistan, a country characterized by rapid digitalization, limited institutional capacity, and evolving legal frameworks. Drawing on qualitative document analysis, case studies, and empirical research, the article analyzes Pakistan's legal and institutional preparedness to address deepfake-related harms across multiple dimensions: legislative adequacy, law enforcement capacity, judicial expertise, and societal awareness. The findings reveal that while Pakistan possesses foundational cybercrime legislation, existing frameworks lack explicit provisions for synthetic media, creating legal ambiguity and enforcement gaps. Law enforcement agencies and courts face significant constraints in detecting, authenticating, and adjudicating deepfake-related cases, compounded by limited forensic infrastructure and specialized training. Societal awareness of deepfake risks remains uneven, with empirical studies indicating that only 10% of respondents were aware of deepfakes and 15% had knowledge of PECA. The article situates these findings within broader debates about AI governance in the Global South, arguing that developing jurisdictions face distinctive vulnerabilities that require context-sensitive policy responses. It concludes by proposing a multi-layered reform agenda encompassing legal clarification, institutional capacity building, technological investment, and public awareness initiatives.
