TI  - [SGINC] BEYOND AWARENESS : AN EMPIRICAL STUDY OF CYBERCRIME IN INDIA
AU  - Ms. Geeta Kumari
AU  - Dr. Ajay Kumar Bhatt
PY  - 2026
JO  - mVedra
DO  - https://doi.org/10.82471/8xe5b-54n23
AB  - [SGINC: empirical study (type mis-tagged)] "Justice delayed is justice denied." -William E. Gladstone. This concern has acquired greater significance in the digital age, where cyber incidents are increasing in both frequency and complexity. Online fraud, impersonation, phishing, identity misuse and other forms of cyber harm affect ordinary users everyday. While greater awareness of cybercrime is essential, but awareness alone may not be enough. A person may know that cybercrime exists and yet remain unsure about recognising a threat, protecting personal information, reporting an incident or seeking the appropriate remedy. This paper examines the gap between general awareness of cybercrime and practical preparedness to deal with it. It adopts a socio-legal approach by combining doctrinal legal analysis with an empirical study of public perceptions and experiences. The findings suggest that knowing about cybercrime does not always mean knowing how to deal with it. Gaps remain in practical guidance, reporting knowledge and confidence in the available redress process. The paper therefore emphasizes the need to make cybercrime awareness more practical by connecting public education with clear legal information, simple reporting pathways and accessible cyber-safety support. Technology can further assist through alerts, guided reporting and appropriate digital redress. AI-enabled ODR may also support suitable lowvalue cyber-related disputes, while serious criminal matters must remain within the formal criminal justice system. Ultimately, meaningful cybercrime awareness should not stop at knowing that a threat exists; it should enable people to know what to do when that threat becomes real.
