AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Abstract Financial fraud has increased rapidly in India due to the advent use if digital payment systems such as the Unified Payments Interface (UPI), the incidence of financial fraud has surged in India. Convenience and speed of digital transactions come with cyber risks such as phishing, fake payment requests, QR code scams, OTP fraud, and fraudulent phone calls. The elderly are especially vulnerable as they often lack knowledge of digital technologies and/or cyber security practices. They are frequently taken aback by fraudsters who take advantage of their lack of technical knowledge, the procedures of digital banking, and their trust. This research paper explores how aware the senior citizens of Mumbai are about the financial scam and the threats of phishing attacks in particular on the UPI system. This study seeks to gain a deeper insight into their awareness of the common tactics used in such a cyber fraud, to identify the factors that leads to cyber victimization, and to assess whether the awareness creation campaigns of banks, government agencies and digital payment platforms are effective. Structured questionnaire was used to gather the primary data for the study among the senior citizens of age 60 years and above residing in different localities of Mumbai. Secondary data were obtained from various sources such as journal, research papers, RBI reports, NPCI publication, newspapers and government websites. Collected data is analysed using statistical tools like percentages, tables and charts. The study is likely to yield important knowledge about the awareness of senior citizens towards digital financial and fraud scams and give recommendations for the improvement of cyber security education, safe digital payment practices and prevention of financial fraud. The results will enable the policymakers, banks, financial institutions and digital payment companies to create more effective financial crimes awareness campaigns for senior citizens.
