AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Abstract This study explores India’s research landscape on cyberbullying, focusing on research trends, collaboration patterns, thematic orientations, and their relationships to cybercrime prevalence across the country. A systematic, data-driven approach was adopted. Data were retrieved from the Scopus database (1999–2025) using search terms recommended by Singh et al. (2024b), yielding 10,974 global publications, of which 1,018 were affiliated with Indian institutions. Bibliometric tools, including Bibliometrix, VOSviewer, PyBibX, CRExplorer, and Microsoft Excel, were used for analysis. At the same time, cybercrime statistics (2018–2022) from the Ministry of Home Affairs were spatially visualised in ArcGIS 10.8.4 using the choropleth method. India contributes 9.25% of the global cyberbullying literature, nearly three times its share in library and information science research. Contributions come from 1,634 institutions across 80 countries, though an average of 2.36 documents per institution indicates uneven participation. Conference proceedings dominate the output, with a collaboration index of 3.69 reflecting moderate co-authorship. The field remains in an emerging phase (H-index = 10; 8.68 citations per paper) but shows a marked surge post-2020, coinciding with increased digital penetration and social awareness. Cybercrime data indicate a parallel rise in incidents, expanding from concentrated hotspots in 2018 to a nationwide concern by 2022. The study relies on Scopus-indexed literature and official cybercrime records, which may exclude certain regional or non-indexed contributions. Future research could integrate alternative databases and qualitative insights. Findings underscore the urgent need for stronger policy frameworks, targeted awareness initiatives, and digital safety measures in India. They also provide evidence to guide researchers, institutions, and policymakers in prioritising resources and collaborative strategies. This is the first comprehensive mapping of India’s scholarly contributions to cyberbullying research, contextualised with real-world cybercrime patterns. By linking bibliometric insights with crime data, the study bridges knowledge production and societal challenges, offering a unique perspective for global and local stakeholders.
