AU  - Kumar Arjit
PY  - 2026
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22849583
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The advent of AI has revolutionised the way in which evidence is presented to Indian courts in criminal trials, allowing the creation of believable audio, video and image evidence to be easily achieved. The deepfake technology reveals a structural flaw in Indian evidence law which evolved with the premise that the photographic and audio-visual record is generally reliable unless there is specific evidence of tampering. This article contends that the problem with deepfakes is not technological, in the sense that it involves the creation of a new technology, but rather it is evidentiary in nature as the present certification procedures under Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, certify the custodial origin of computer output but do not verify whether the content in the computer output is itself a work of art or a construct. This article compares and contrasts Indian procedural and evidentiary law to authentic versus synthetic evidence and the European Union, the United Kingdom, the United States and Singapore to determine if Indian law provides the courts with the means to differentiate between authentic and synthetic evidence. It concludes that incremental judicial correction is not enough and recommends statutory changes: Content-authentication protocols, Specialized evidentiary presumptions, and Judicial & Investigational training to preserve Article 21 of the constitution (right to a fair trial).
