TI  - [SGINC] Deepfake Regulations in India: Challenges and Way Forward
AU  - Namisha Chadha
AU  - Shiv Johar
PY  - 2026
JO  - International Journal of Legal Science and Innovation
DO  - https://doi.org/10.63108/ijlsi.7064
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Deepfakes are synthetically generated information (SGI) that pose a fundamental challenge to the visual distinction between real and fake. According to the Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology’s FAQs on the 2026 amendment to the IT Rules, the advent of artificial intelligence (“AI”) has made it substantially easier “to create, generate, modify or alter highly realistic synthetic audio, visual and audio-visual content, including deepfakes, that can appear real, authentic or true and may mislead or deceive users.” It is therefore possible to give the deceptive impression that an individual has said, done, performed or endorsed something that never happened. Such information can be misused to cause serious harm through misinformation, impersonation, identity fraud, non-consensual intimate imagery, obscenity, child sexual exploitative and abuse material, reputational harm, coercion, extortion, privacy violations and the commercial exploitation of identity. The biggest challenge today is that there is no coherent substantive framework governing the underlying right to be protected against synthetic identity appropriation, and remedies are dispersed across various areas of law. India’s legal landscape has evolved partially through the IT Act, the IT Rules, the BNS, the DPDP Act, copyright law, passing-off principles, defamation law and constitutional privacy jurisprudence. Indian courts have also developed a body of personality-rights jurisprudence, particularly concerning celebrities. The regulatory position changed significantly in February 2026, when the IT Rules were amended to define and regulate “synthetically generated information,” including deepfakes. The amendments strengthen user awareness and accountability, expedite the takedown of illegal content, and introduce due diligence obligations requiring intermediaries to ensure labelling, provenance and technical verification for permissible SGI. They represent a significant pivot from purely reactive content moderation towards proactive platform regulation. Despite these reforms, a deeper substantive question remains unaddressed: what legal right does an individual possess over the digital reproduction, dissemination and manipulation of her or his identity? This paper proposes that India would benefit greatly from a technology-neutral framework that combines the 2026 intermediary regime for SGI with clear and codified digital personality rights, explicit protection against non-consensual synthetic intimate imagery, expedited victim remedies, proportionate obligations across the AI creation and distribution chain, robust provenance mechanisms, and balanced safeguards for satire, journalism, parody, political criticism and artistic expression, so that freedom of speech is not stifled.
