AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] While "crime" lacks a single statutory definition, it is generally understood as an act or omission forbidden by law and subject to punishment.Jurists view it as a social wrong that can only be pardoned by the state, not private individuals.Notably, Roscoe Pound argued that a fixed definition is impossible because law is constantly evolving.A significant shift in the criminal landscape is the rise of cybercrime-including hacking, identity theft, and "digital arrest"-as well as an increase in offenses committed by juveniles (those under 18).While Indian laws like the Information Technology Act (2000) and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (2023) address these offenses, they do not provide an exhaustive definition of cybercrime itself.Because juveniles are often protected due to a lack of criminal intent, the focus remains on rehabilitation.However, as technology advances, traditional methods are becoming outdated.To effectively reintegrate young offenders, the system must adopt modern interventions, such as virtual reality (VR), mobile applications, and wearable monitoring devices.
