TI  - [SGINC] Cybercrime Investigation and Admissibility of Electronic Evidence in India
AU  - Apurva Bajpai
AU  - Sanjeev Sharma
PY  - 2026
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23470233
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Electronic evidence connects cybercrime investigation with adjudication, but legal recognition of digital records does not establish their integrity, authorship or truth. This article examines the Indian framework through a doctrinal analysis of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam 2023, the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita 2023 and the judicial development of electronic-evidence certification. It argues that admissibility should be understood as one stage in an evidentiary process extending from lawful acquisition to meaningful courtroom challenge. Particular attention is given to the savings provision preserving the earlier Evidence Act for specified pending matters, the relationship between primary electronic records and computer output, and the two-part certificate prescribed by section 63. The Supreme Court’s May 2026 order in Pune Bar Association is examined without treating its qualified observations about expert certification as a final resolution of every interpretive question. The article develops a record-specific approach integrating provenance, documented transformations, appropriately qualified expertise and fair disclosure. It concludes that neither formal certification nor technological verification can replace judicial evaluation: trustworthy electronic proof requires a demonstrable connection between the record collected, the exhibit produced and the factual inference sought.
