AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This paper asks whether India's existing legal protections are adequate to address AI-generated deepfakes that exploit a person's face, voice or likeness without consent. It distinguishes personality rights from the neighbouring concepts of privacy and publicity rights, and traces the judicial development of personality rights in India — from the passing-off era (D.M. Entertainment, ICC Development, Titan Industries, Shivaji Rao Gaikwad) through the constitutional recognition of privacy in Puttaswamy, to the recent wave of digital-exploitation cases (Amitabh Bachchan v. Rajat Nagi, Anil Kapoor, Arijit Singh) that directly confront AI-based impersonation. The paper then maps the fragmented statutory landscape — the Information Technology Act's provisions on personation and obscenity, the 2026 Amendment Rules on synthetically generated information, the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, copyright and performers' rights, and trademark/passing-off remedies — and identifies gaps around non-commercial replicas, cross-border enforcement, and protection for ordinary (non-celebrity) individuals. Weighing these concerns against free-speech protections under Article 19, it argues that Indian law has developed a workable but judge-made, celebrity-centred and fragmented framework, and proposes statutory recognition of personality rights, graded regulation of AI impersonation, faster takedown mechanisms, and protections extending beyond public figures.
