AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Abstract: Cybercrime has emerged as one of the most critical challenges of the digital era, posing substantial threats to individuals, businesses, and governments worldwide. This study presents a state wise comparative analysis of cybercrime patterns in India, one of the most populated country in world. The primary objective is to examine variations in cybercrime incidents across selected states of India and to identify significant temporal and regional differences in cybercrime trends. The analysis is based on cybercrime data collected from the top ten states with the highest reported incidences in India, over multiple years. Statistical techniques, including Analysis of Variance (ANOVA), are employed to evaluate differences in cybercrime rates across states and time periods. In cases where the assumptions of ANOVA are not satisfied, appropriate non-parametric alternatives are utilized to ensure robust inference. The study further investigates the influence of selected socio-demographic and crime-related factors, such as population density, fraud, personal revenge, sexual exploitation and other motives associated with cyber offences. By comparing the spatial and temporal distribution of cybercrime among the states, the research provides insights into emerging patterns, regional disparities and underlying determinants of cybercrime. The findings contribute to a better understanding of cybercrime dynamics and may assist policymakers, law enforcement agencies and cyber security practitioners in developing effective prevention and mitigation strategies. Keywords: Cybercrime; Comparative Analysis; India; Analysis of Variance (ANOVA); Non-Parametric Tests; Cyber security; Crime Statistics; Regional Disparities; Temporal Trends; Fraud; Digital Crime Analytics.
