AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This paper examines political deepfakes in India and their threats to democratic integrity, electoral fairness, and public trust. AI-generated media can impersonate public figures, distort evidence, and spread rapidly during elections, revealing the inadequacy of the current laws, which are reactive. These rely on notice and takedown obligations that often lack transparency, oversight, and capacity to handle scale, speed, and linguistic diversity. Drawing on comparative experiences from the US, EU, and UK, which combine differentiated platform duties, procedural safeguards, and independent oversight, the paper advocates a proportional, gatekeeper sensitive approach. A multilayered governance model integrating command and control regulation, co and self regulation, and independent oversight is proposed to balance free expression, electoral integrity, and informational trust, providing an anticipatory, rights-sensitive framework for addressing political deepfakes in India.
