AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Deepfake technology, which is synthetic audiovisual media developed using deep learning architecture (including Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) and diffusion models), has transitioned from technical novelty to one of the most important sociotechnical developments of the early 21st century. The impact of deepfakes on society is explored in this chapter in terms of cybersecurity, education, and training; digital humans; digital immortality; media literacy; and adolescent mental health. Using peer-reviewed articles, court documents, government research initiatives, and data from industry, published between 2019 and 2025, as its sources, this chapter concludes that deepfakes are neither inherently harmful nor definitively helpful. Instead, they are amplifiers of existing social conditions, reflecting both the opportunity for harm and the opportunity for benefit. This chapter connects deepfake phenomena to the neuroscientific constructs of perception articulated by Roswandowitz and colleagues at the University of Zurich, analyzes the degree to which adolescent brain development renders adolescents especially susceptible to abuse of synthetic media, and discusses real examples of deepfake abuse in Hong Kong, Argentina, the Philippines, India, Spain, and South Korea. This chapter closes with a responsible governance framework consisting of origin infrastructure, tiered legal structures, universal media literacy, and victim services.
