AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Abstract This review examines the evolution of cybercrime and cyber law in response to the rapid development of information and communication technologies. The increasing use of the internet, digital payment systems, social media, cloud computing and interconnected devices has created new opportunities for criminal activity while presenting complex challenges to legal systems. The study aims to analyze the concept, characteristics and changing forms of cybercrime; examine the development of national and international cyber-law frameworks; and identify key legal, procedural and institutional challenges. The paper adopts a literature-based legal review approach, examining academic literature, legislation, judicial decisions, international legal instruments and other relevant legal materials, with particular attention to the Indian legal system and comparative international perspectives. It discusses cyber offenses such as unauthorized access, online fraud, identity theft, cyberstalking, malware, ransomware and attacks on critical infrastructure. It also considers emerging technological challenges involving artificial intelligence, cryptocurrencies, deepfakes, cloud computing and the Internet of Things. The review identifies several recurring challenges, including jurisdictional uncertainty, cross border investigations, difficulties in collecting and authenticating digital evidence, inconsistent legal frameworks, limited technical expertise, underreporting and gaps between legislation and effective enforcement. It further examines the tension between expanded investigative powers and the protection of privacy, freedom of expression, due process and other fundamental rights. The analysis highlights the importance of international cooperation, effective evidentiary procedures, institutional capacity and rights-based safeguards in responding to cybercrime. The review concludes that the effectiveness of cyber law depends not merely on legislative development but also on practical implementation, technological preparedness and cooperation among jurisdictions. It identifies a need for greater empirical and comparative research, particularly concerning enforcement outcomes and India’s evolving legal framework. It recommends continuous legal review, interdisciplinary research, capacity building and technology-neutral legislation supported by clear definitions and procedural safeguards.
