AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid growth of digital technology has led to an increase in cyber threats, among which ransomware attacks have become one of the growing challenges in the digital environment. Ransomware is a type of cyberattack in which criminals gain unauthorised access to computer systems, restrict access to data and demand payment from victims. Such attacks affect individuals, businesses and public institutions. The anonymity of attackers, the use of digital payment methods and cross-border issues make it difficult to identify offenders and establish criminal responsibility. This research examines the legal challenges faced in India, including difficulties in identifying offenders, collecting and proving digital evidence, and conducting cross-border investigations. Although ransomware is not recognised as a separate offence under Indian cyber laws, the activities involved in ransomware attacks may fall within the scope of existing laws such as the Information Technology Act, 2000 and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023. However, the evolving nature of ransomware raises questions about the extent to which these provisions can be applied to such offences. The study examines whether existing Indian legal provisions can effectively cover ransomware attacks through appropriate interpretation. It also undertakes a comparative study of the legal framework of the United States to understand how ransomware attacks are addressed there and to examine the position of the Indian legal framework. The study adopts a doctrinal research methodology, analysing relevant statutory provisions, judicial decisions and legal principles relating to cyber offences. It assesses whether current Indian laws are adequate to address ransomware attacks effectively or whether they require clarification to respond to ransomware-related offences.
