AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The sudden digitalization of governance and communication in India has created new avenues for cybercrime which was not foreseen. One such emerging phenomenon is known as Digital Arrest. Which is a form of cyber fraud where criminals pose themselves as law enforcement officials and trick people into believing through video calls, emails or messages that they are in trouble by being arrested for serious offences. By preying on fear, ignorance of the law and the power to do so that comes with police institutions fraudsters extract money and sensitive information from unsuspecting victims. This article is a critical analysis of the concept of digital arrest as it was pointed out that there is no legal recognition of arrest in India's laws. It analyses the legal framework currently in place to arrest relevant constitutional safeguards and the applicability of criminal and cyber laws in respect of such frauds. The article goes on to assess judicial interpretations analyze the loopholes in enforcement and examine recent developments in cybercrime regulations. The article ends with a proposal for legal, institutional and societal measures to check the menace of increasing number of digital arrest scams in India.
