AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] India’s digital revolution has been accompanied by an equally dramatic surge in cybercrime. The enactment of three new criminal laws, the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (BNS), the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (BNSS), and the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 (BSA), effective from July 1, 2024, has fundamentally overhauled the country’s criminal justice architecture. These laws now operate in conjunction with the Information Technology Act, 2000 (IT Act), which remains the primary legislation governing cybercrimes in India. This paper critically examines the relationship between the IT Act and the new criminal laws, identifying areas of legislative overlap, structural conflict, and significant gaps in protection against online harms, particularly in the context of emerging threats such as deepfakes, AI-generated content, organised cybercrime, and digital privacy violations. Drawing on statutory analysis, judicial precedents, and empirical data, the paper argues that while the new criminal laws represent a meaningful modernisation effort, India’s cyber law framework remains fragmented, definitionally ambiguous, and structurally inadequate to meet the demands of the contemporary digital threat landscape. The paper concludes with recommendations for legislative reform, including the urgent need for a consolidated, technology-neutral cybercrime statute.
