AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The proliferation of AI-generated deepfakes has evolved as a very serious concern in developing countries like India and even in developed countries like the United States in recent years. Whereas this technological advancement is harmful for individual privacy, it diminishes the public trust in the media institutions and makes public discourse even more complex. In recent years governments and regulatory institutions have started to recognise these challenges, the legal and ethical frameworks remain fragmented and uneven across different nations. This study explores a cross-national comparison of India and the United States, emphasizes differences in legal, ethical, and media governance responses to deepfakes. Using secondary sources such as laws, policy documents, case studies, journalistic accounts, and academic research, this paper examines how India and the United States are dealing with challenges of platform accountability, regulatory gaps, public perception, media ethics, and digital media governance. The study explores how India and the United States follow different legislative and media governance approaches, but both face common concerns regarding misinformation, victim protection, mental health, and the promotion of media literacy. The findings of the study suggest that there is a need for a global regulatory framework which balances technological innovation with accountability. It also stresses the significance of ethical journalism and media literacy in addressing the misuse of deepfakes.
