AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This study examines the impact of digital public infrastructure (DPI) and economic growth on cybercrime across 28 Indian states during the period 2015-2023. A composite DPI index is constructed using principal component analysis (PCA). System GMM, Tapio decoupling, logarithmic mean divisia index (LMDI) decomposition approaches are employed for untangling the nexus between DPI, economic growth and cybercrime. System GMM results show that 1% increase in DPI index raises the cybercrime rate by about 1%. While the effect of unemployment varies across model specifications, police strength, inflation, and poverty do not exhibit statistically significant effects. The results of the Tapio decoupling approach demonstrate the decoupling of recorded cybercrime from economic growth and DPI expansion in many Indian states suggesting that economic growth and DPI expansion do not necessarily lead to proportional increase in cybercrime. The LMDI decomposition results demonstrate the larger role of DPI than state gross domestic product per capita in increasing cybercrime rate in most of the states. The findings highlight the need for investments in cybersecurity, regulation, and state-specific institutional capacity building alongside DPI expansion.
