TI  - [SGINC] Beyond Authentication: A Three-Pillar Evidentiary Framework for Deepfake Evidence in Court of law
AU  - Pournima Inamdar
PY  - 2026
JO  - Journal of Intelligent Decision Making and Information Science
DO  - https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v3.1771
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] As Artificial Intelligence—generated deepfakes become forensically indistinguishable from authentic media, existing rules of evidence including the United States Federal Rules of Evidence 1975, the United Kingdom's Police and Criminal Evidence Act 1984, and India's Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 are proving inadequate to ensure the reliability of audiovisual evidence in criminal proceedings. This article conducts a comparative doctrinal analysis of evidentiary authentication frameworks across these three jurisdictions, demonstrating that current standards were designed for an era in which media manipulation left detectable forensic artefacts. We identify a dual epistemic threat: the risk of admitting fabricated deepfake evidence (the "fabrication threat") and the risk of excluding authentic evidence through spurious deepfake claims (the "liar's dividend"). To address this doctrinal gap, we propose a three-pillar jurisprudential model comprising (i) a structured burden-shifting authentication protocol, (ii) judicial notice of AI generative capability, and (iii) a provenance-first evidentiary doctrine grounded in cryptographic content credentials. The model is evaluated against the constitutional and procedural constraints of each jurisdiction, with particular attention to the Indian evidentiary landscape under the newly enacted Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam. We conclude with concrete legislative and procedural reform recommendations for courts operating in the post-authentication era.
