AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid spread of generative artificial intelligence has placed synthetic media at the centre of the Indian privacy debate. Deepfakes, understood as audio-visual content generated or altered by machine learning models so as to depict a real person in situations that never happened, have intensified these concerns. This paper analyses how deepfakes are handled in Indian law today and why that falls short of what is needed. It examines the Information Technology Act, 2000, the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 and the IT Amendment Rules of 2025 and 2026, along with the personality-rights orders of the Delhi and Bombay High Courts. None of these laws was written with generative AI in mind. The largest obstacle is section 3(c)(ii) of the DPDP Act, which places personal data made publicly available by a person outside the Act altogether, and the Act's consent-based fiduciary model cannot reach anonymous or cross-border generative actors. The paper argues that reform must rest on a dignitary reading of Article 21 rather than an extension of consent doctrine, and proposes a four-tier framework of constitutional recognition, statutory amendment, ex-ante technical duties and modernised remedies, disciplined by the standard in Shreya Singhal v. Union of India.
