AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid development of Artificial Intelligence has significantly transformed the landscape of cybercrime by enabling sophisticated, automated large scale digital offences that frequently challenge traditional legal and regulatory frameworks. This chapter examines the growing intersection between AI and Cybercrime, with a specific focus on emerging threats such as deepfakes, AI powered Phishing, automated ransomware attacks and identity fraud. It depicts the rapid spike in the use of AI models in cybercrimes with increased efficiency and complexity of cybercrimes. The chapter critically analyses the inadequacy of current cyberlaws within the Indian legal framework while addressing AI enabled offences. It also evaluates the limitations of the Information Technology Act, 2000 and the existing cybersecurity governance mechanism in responding to the challenges related to accountability, cross border enforcement, privacy and transparency. Further, the chapter undertakes a comparative examination of global regulatory approaches, including the European Union’s AI governance model, Innovation oriented frameworks by United States and China’s state centric regulatory system. The chapter emphasises that the evolving nature of AI-driven cybercrime necessitates comprehensive and dynamic legal reforms that balance the technological advancement with cybersecurity, privacy protection and human rights.
