AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid expansion of digital technologies has transformed economic activities, governance structures, and social interactions across the member states of the Organization of Islamic Cooperation (OIC). While digital transformation has generated significant opportunities for innovation and development, it has also increased exposure to cybercrime, cyber espionage, data breaches, cyberterrorism, and other transnational cyber threats. In response, OIC countries have increasingly adopted cybercrime legislation and digital governance policies aimed at protecting national security, critical infrastructure, and digital sovereignty. This study examines the evolving relationship between cybercrime regulation and digital sovereignty in selected OIC countries, focusing on emerging legal trends, regulatory challenges, and future governance frameworks.Employing a qualitative doctrinal and comparative legal methodology, the research analyzes cybercrime laws, cybersecurity policies, data governance regulations, judicial approaches, and international legal instruments in countries including Pakistan, Malaysia, Indonesia, Saudi Arabia, and Türkiye. The study explores key issues such as cross-border cybercrime, data localization, artificial intelligence-related security risks, cloud computing dependency, digital surveillance, and state authority in cyberspace. The findings reveal that while OIC countries have made significant progress in strengthening cybercrime regulation, considerable differences remain regarding enforcement mechanisms, institutional capacity, cybersecurity strategies, and approaches to digital sovereignty. Furthermore, growing dependence on foreign digital platforms and technologies presents new challenges to national regulatory control and data governance.The study argues that effective cyber governance in OIC countries requires a balanced approach that simultaneously protects national sovereignty, promotes digital innovation, safeguards fundamental rights, and enhances international cooperation. It proposes greater legal harmonization, stronger regional cybersecurity collaboration, and the development of an OIC-oriented framework for digital governance capable of addressing emerging cyber threats while supporting sustainable digital transformation. The research contributes to contemporary debates on cyber law, digital governance, and state sovereignty in an increasingly interconnected digital environment.
