AB  - [SGEXC: wrong field] The proliferation of online exploitation in India, encompassing cyberstalking, image-based sexual abuse, and the production, distribution, and consumption of child sexual abuse material (CSAM), has surged significantly due to enhanced connection. This article provides a doctrinal and policy study of India’s developing legal response, contrasting the legal protections afforded to women, who necessitate gender-sensitive safeguards, with those for children, who require age-based, capacity-sensitive, and strict-liability protections. We examine the primary statutes: the Information Technology Act, 2000 (IT Act), the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012 (POCSO), and the recent Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (BNS), along with the platform-oriented Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021 (IT Rules 2021). We delineate overlaps and deficiencies in offenses, procedures, intermediary liability, and remedies, utilizing practice notes and international advice. The study reveals that Indian law has strong provisions against CSAM and a range of offenses aimed at protecting gender and children (e.g., voyeurism, stalking); nonetheless, it continues to encounter challenges in enforcement, inconsistent takedown processes, evidential obstacles, and jurisdictional conflicts with transnational platforms. It advocates for more explicit duediligence deadlines for emergency takedown, victim-focused recompense, enhanced default product design, and cohesive cyber forensics capabilities. The four models of Machine Learning implement into the particular dataset which shows the Random Forest shows the best accuracy which is 93.2.
