AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] ABSTRACT Cybercrime has become one of the most important legal and ethical issues of the digital era and has necessitated the creation of robust legal systems to deal with the ever-changing nature of cyber technology. This study aims to compare the Islamic and modern legal systems, in their approach to cybercrime, highlighting the bases of law, goals, mechanisms and current relevance of the two legal systems. The sources of authority for Islamic law are the Qur'an, the Sunnah, Ijma', Qiyas, and Ijtihad, and for modern legal systems, the primary sources of authority are the Constitution, legislative enactments, judicial precedents, and international legal instruments. This article critically explores how the concept of Maqasid al-Shariah can be applied to tackle cyber related crimes like identity theft, data breaches, online fraud, misinformation, cyber harassment, digital privacy violation, and threat of artificial intelligence. It holds that the goals of Shariah, especially religion, life, intellect, lineage and property, make a complete ethical and legal guideline for the regulation of digital activities and protection of human dignity. In addition, the study examines the merits and demerits of the existing cyber legislation such as constitutional protections, privacy laws, and cybercrime laws, especially in the context of Pakistan's legislation. It examines how Islamic law principles intersect with the modern laws and statutes, noting the similarities and differences between them in terms of legislation, human rights, ethics and digital governance. The research also covers the emerging technologies including artificial intelligence, blockchain, deepfakes, ransomware and cross-border cybercrimes, and the importance of changing interpretation and continuous legal reform. The study ends its examination of Islamic law and modern law by stating that both are incapable on their own to be able to solve the cybercrime issues. An effective harmonisation of Islamic law principles, specifically Maqasid al-Shariah, with existing legal systems and tools can provide a better, more ethical, and sustainable approach to combatting cybercrime and ensure justice, digital security, privacy, and the protection of fundamental human rights in the digital age. Keywords: Cybercrime, Islamic Law, Modern Legal System, Maqasid al-Shariah, Digital Governance
