AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The most rapid growth and expansion of information and communication technologies, artificial intelligence, cloud computing, and electronic commerce has drastically transformed the modern society. At the same time, the cybercrime has emerged as one of the most serious threats to the individuals, institutions affecting the businesses, and governments. Traditional criminal law frameworks largely inadequate to address technologically sophisticated offences such as phishing, identity theft, cryptocurrency fraud, cyberstalking, and data breaches etc., The Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (BNS) .seeks to modernize India's substantive criminal law framework and address contemporary forms of criminality. While cybercrime continues to be principally regulated through the Information Technology Act, 2000, The BNS supplements the legal framework by introducing provisions relevant to digital offences, organized cybercrime, financial fraud, and technology-enabled criminal conduct. This paper critically examines the role of the BNS in cybercrime regulation, evaluates its strengths and weaknesses and identifies legal, constitutional, issues and challenges associated with enforcement in the digital era.
