AU  - Hafiz Omer Abdullah
AU  - Muhammad Imran Darwaish
AU  - Dr Mazhar Jamal Chaudhry
AU  - Mohsin Ehsan
PY  - 2026
JO  - Journal of Global Social Transformation
DO  - https://doi.org/10.71317/jgst.2.5.2026.499
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The current study focused on implications of artificial intelligence generated deep fakes on authenticity and admissibility of digital evidence and safeguarding of fair-trial rights in Pakistan. It examined the relationships among perceived deep fake threat, institutional/forensic readiness, legal-procedural protections, and authenticity/confidence in admissibility and fair-trial protections. The positivist philosophy and deductive method were used in this study, along with quantitative cross sectional explanatory design. A self-structured five-point Likert scale questionnaire was used to gather data with 300 respondents, which comprised judges, judicial officers, practicing advocates, public prosecutors, investigators, cybercrime officers and digital-forensic experts. The data were analyzed by SPSS software on frequencies, percentages, mean, standard deviation, and Pearson correlation and regression analysis. The results showed a very high perception of the threat from deep fakes, including fabricated evidence, incorrect decisions by judges, and falsified recordings of real evidence. Institutional and forensic readiness was considered to be low, due to lack of training, access to qualified experts, laboratory capacity and lack of validated detection tools. The results of the regressions confirmed the significant negative effect of perceived deep fake threat on authenticity and the admissibility of confidence. In contrast, there was a significant increase in evidentiary confidence of institutional and forensic readiness. Legal-procedural protections had a significant positive impact on perceived fair trial protections, and authenticity and admissibility confidence significantly contributed to perceptions of procedural fairness. There was support for all four research hypotheses. The study found that the visual realism method was no longer sufficient in determining the authenticity of digital evidence. Pakistan needed evidences-driven guidelines for AI, certified forensic labs, expert training, preservation of original documents and metadata, clear expert reporting, de/judicial examination, and sound judgments. For the purposes of maintaining digital evidence and assuring fair trial rights, a multi-layered technical capacity and procedural protections were required.
