AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This study explored the moderating role of security behaviour-efficacy and the mediating role of financial scam susceptibility in the link between factors influencing financial fraud victimization among the adult users of digital financial services in Karachi, Pakistan. The study concentrated on those who used mobile banking, online banking, digital wallets and digital payment systems, as they access digital finance platforms more regularly, making them more susceptible to phishing, fake investment offers, unauthorised payment requests and other scam tactics. A quantitative survey design was used, and primary data were gathered using an online questionnaire via Google Forms. A total of 336 valid responses were collected using non-probability convenience sampling in Karachi. PLS-SEM and PLS-MGA were used to analyse data. Results indicated that financial scam susceptibility was significantly lower when participants had high levels of digital financial literacy and financial knowledge, but not when they had high levels of objective numeracy. Financial fraud victimisation was a significant increase in scam susceptibility, which in turn is an important financial behavioural pathway for how less financial competence contributes to increased financial fraud risk. The indirect-effect analysis also supported the role of scam susceptibility as a mediator of the effect of DFIL and financial knowledge on the outcome of fraud victimisation, which was not the case for objective numeracy. The results of moderation indicated that financial knowledge was significantly moderated by education, monthly income, and security behaviour self-efficacy, and not moderated by age. Findings from the Gender-based Multi-Group Analysis showed that there were significant differences in selected relationships: digital financial literacy and scam susceptibility, scam susceptibility and fraud victimisation, and financial knowledge and fraud victimization indirectly via scam susceptibility. The results indicate that financial scam prevention in the digital financial services sector in Karachi should rely on financial literacy, digital safety education, behavioural warning, transaction verification, and gender sensitive awareness campaigns to minimise the vulnerability to fraud and safeguard consumers from financial fraud. Keywords: Financial Competencies, Scam Susceptibility, Scam Victimisation, PLS-MGA, Pakistan.
