TI  - [SGINC] ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AND ONLINE FRAUD IN INDIA: LEGAL, TECHNOLOGICAL AND REGULATORY CHALLENGES AND THE NEED FOR A RISK-BASED FRAMEWORK
AU  - Nilam Pawar
PY  - 2026
JO  - LawFoyer International Journal of Doctrinal Legal Research.
DO  - https://doi.org/10.70183/lijdlr.2026.v04.383
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid development of artificial intelligence has transformed the nature, scale and sophistication of online fraud. Artificial intelligence is increasingly used not only to automate conventional cyber-fraud techniques but also to create new forms of deception involving voice cloning, deepfakes, synthetic identities, automated phishing, AI-generated messages, impersonation, fraudulent customer-service interactions and highly personalised social-engineering attacks. These developments challenge legal frameworks that were principally designed around human-generated communications and conventional forms of cybercrime. India possesses a substantial body of law capable of addressing AI-enabled fraud, including the Information Technology Act 2000 and the Rules framed under it, the three criminal-law codes of 2023, the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023 and the Consumer Protection Act 2019. These instruments do not, however, constitute a unified regulatory framework. Questions of attribution, evidentiary authenticity, platform responsibility, bank liability, cross-border investigation and the allocation of liability between users, developers, intermediaries and financial institutions remain insufficiently resolved. This paper argues that the principal legal challenge is not the use of AI itself but the use of AI to create deception that causes, or is reasonably capable of causing, legally cognisable harm. It therefore proposes a risk-based and technology-neutral framework built on four principles: prevention, traceability, proportionate liability and effective victim redress. The paper examines the constitutional implications under Articles 14, 19 and 21, the evidentiary challenges associated with AI-generated material, intermediary liability and comparative regulatory approaches in the European Union, United States, United Kingdom, China and Singapore. The paper concludes that India does not require a general prohibition on AI-generated content. It requires a coordinated framework that combines criminal law, financial-fraud prevention, data protection, intermediary obligations, AI transparency, provenance technologies, rapid freezing of fraudulent transactions and specialised digital-evidence standards.
