AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Abstract: The rapid expansion of the digital economy has resulted in an exponential growth in data generation, storage, and processing, transforming data into a critical asset for organizations worldwide. While digital transformation has significantly improved operational efficiency, innovation, and global connectivity, it has simultaneously increased exposure to cybersecurity threats, particularly data breaches. Data breaches involve unauthorized access, disclosure, or loss of sensitive personal and corporate information, leading to severe consequences such as identity theft, financial fraud, reputational damage, and loss of consumer trust. More importantly, they give rise to complex legal implications, including civil liability, criminal prosecution, regulatory penalties, and contractual disputes. This paper critically examines the legal implications of data breaches within the framework of the digital economy, with a focus on prominent global and national regulatory regimes such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and India’s Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act). The paper highlights the need for robust data governance frameworks, proactive cybersecurity strategies, and international cooperation to effectively address the risks associated with data breaches and ensure the protection of individual privacy rights in the digital age. Keywords: Data Breach, Digital Economy, Cybersecurity, Data Protection Law, GDPR, DPDP Act 2023, Legal Liability, Privacy Rights, Regulatory Compliance, Information Security
