AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rise of generative AI has triggered a surge in non-consensual deepfakes—synthetic media used for revenge porn, sexualized morphing, and intimate image abuse—inflicting severe gender-based harm. Globally and in India, 96–98% of deepfake content is non-consensual pornographic material, with 99% of victims being women and girls. This technology-facilitated violence perpetuates digital misogyny, causing psychological trauma, reputational damage, social ostracism, and economic exclusion, compounded by societal stigma that silences victims. In India, the Right to Personality—protecting dignity, privacy, and autonomy over one's likeness under Article 21 of the Constitution, as affirmed in Justice K.S. Puttaswamy v Union of India (2017)—faces existential challenge from AI-enabled deepfakes. The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 requires consent for biometric data processing and imposes fines up to ₹250 crore for breaches, yet it overlooks synthetic manipulation. Complementary provisions in the Information Technology Act, 2000 (ss 66E, 67), Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, and intermediary rules offer partial remedies but lack standalone criminalization of deepfake creation. Employing doctrinal and comparative methods, this paper examines whether personality rights can endure this threat. It reviews judicial injunctions against morphed content and the Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Amendment Rules, 2026 (effective February 20, 2026), which define synthetically generated information (SGI), mandate labelling/metadata, and enforce 2–3 hour takedowns for non-consensual intimate imagery. Findings highlight gaps: reliance on general laws, enforcement hurdles (anonymity, cross-border issues), and cultural barriers to reporting. Comparative models—the EU AI Act (high-risk classification), US TAKE IT DOWN Act (criminalizing NCII), and UK Online Safety Act—suggest reforms. The paper concludes that without targeted legislation criminalizing creation/dissemination, gender-sensitive provisions, mandatory AI detection/watermarking, victim redress, and platform accountability, personality rights risk erosion. Urgent action is vital to protect women's dignity in India's digital space. Keywords: Deepfakes, non-consensual intimate imagery, personality rights, gender-based violence, revenge porn, sexualized morphing, Article 21, Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023, IT Rules 2026, India.
