AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Digital evidence has assumed the position of the backbone of the modern criminal justice system on account of the contemporary proliferation of cybercrime, yet several unaccounted factors pertaining to its alteration, duplication and even deletion has not been able to save it from being perpetually questioned on grounds of reliability. This paper examines the exact question by the synthesis of three recent scholarly studies, taking into consideration the narrative review of the legal and forensic dimensions of electronic evidence, the doctrinal study of the Indian criminal legal background, especially post-enactment of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (Hereinafter referred to as 'BSA') and the qualitative study of the legal standards and the practices of chain-of-custody across several jurisdictions. This paper attempts to make a distinguishing premise between 'admissibility', which serves as a threshold question and 'reliability', which serves as the weight deserved by an admission. It makes an analysis of the authentication, chain-of-custody, expert testimony, cognitive bias and cross-border collection and procedural fairness as the core factors for determining reliability. It also traces the evolution of Indian criminal justice's certification requirement ranging from section 65B of the Indian Evidence Act (herein after referred to as 'IEA'), 1872 to section 63 of the BSA, 2023. Another additional consideration discussed here is the re-moulding of forensic practice by Artificial Intelligence, Blockchain, Internet of Things and Deepfake Detection. The paper also advances an argument that reliability cannot only be secured by mere legal codification, rather ought to be matched by standardised forensic protocols, adequate laboratory capacity and judicial training. As a final declaration, this paper would also like to caution that some of the empirical data set and case-law related material upon which, due reliance has been placed, derive their source from secondary c...
