AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid development of Artificial Intelligence (AI) has transformed communication, education, employment, social interaction and access to information. At the same time, AI has created new possibilities for cyber harassment and technology-facilitated abuse. Traditional forms of online harassment, including threatening messages, cybers talking, impersonation, doxing and non-consensual dissemination of private material, can now be produced and distributed with greater speed and sophistication through AI-enabled tools. Generative AI has further complicated the problem by making it possible to create realistic but fabricated photographs, videos, audio recordings and written communications. Deepfakes, AI-generated sexual imagery, automated impersonation, targeted harassment and sophisticated phishing or social-engineering campaigns represent emerging risks for individuals, particularly women, children, journalists, public figures and other vulnerable groups. The relationship between AI and cyber harassment is not entirely negative. AI can also assist in detecting abusive content, identifying coordinated harassment, authenticating digital evidence, improving reporting mechanisms and supporting law-enforcement investigations. The central challenge is therefore not AI itself but the manner in which AI systems are designed, accessed, deployed and regulated. This article examines the changing nature of cyber harassment in the AI era, identifies its principal forms, analyses potential advantages and disadvantages of AI, considers legal and institutional responses, and proposes recommendations for governments, courts, law-enforcement agencies, educational institutions, technology companies and civil society. Particular attention is given to Pakistan, where the expansion of digital participation has increased the importance of effective responses to technology-facilitated gender-based violence. Pakistan adopted the Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act, 2016 (PECA), subsequently amended in 2025, while the country also developed its first National Strategy to Counter Technology-Facilitated Gender-Based Violence in 2026. The article argues that effective protection requires a combination of law reform, digital literacy, technological safeguards, survivor-centred justice, institutional capacity and responsible AI governance.
