AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] India's fast pace of digitalization has triggered an explosive surge in cybercrime cases, while current reporting systems remain mostly manual, text-based, and are not designed to triage and preserve evidence in real time. In this paper, a prototype multimodal AI framework, named IntelliReport Cyber, is introduced that allows for structured cybercrime reporting, automated categorization, and severity based prioritization for the Indian scenario. Complaints are fed into the system in both text and voice, with both the complaints and the model languages being code-mixed, and the system uses the Hinglish-aware transformer models, namely HingRoBERTa and HingBERT. Multi-Task Learning (MTL) architecture classifies cybercrime type while predicting the severity (Low/Medium/High/Critical), which lets you triage high risk cases in the early stages. A grid-search is performed over the values of$\lambda \mathbf{c}=\mathbf{0. 3}, \mathbf{0. 5}, \mathbf{0. 7}$and$\lambda \mathbf{s}=\mathbf{1}- \lambda \mathbf{c}$, and the configuration with the best performance is chosen on the validation set. The framework is enhanced with a rule-based legal mapping, based on Indian cyber law, and a guided forensic evidence collection module. Evaluation is carried out on an Indian cybercrime complaint dataset where class imbalance issues are taken into account using synthetic augmentation techniques such as back-translating, paraphrasing, etc. for minority classes like ransomware and impersonation. The performance of the speechto-text component is measured with Word Error Rate (WER) with a 12.4% WER on Hinglish voice input. Results show a consistent performance on all types of crimes, and are sharper results in the case of handling Hinglish complaint compared with baseline multilingual models. The topics of privacy, ethical concerns, and feasibility of deployment are also addressed. The proposed framework emphasizes the role of AI in multimodal reporting as a valuable tool to further the benefits of cyber governance by reducing reporting delays, increasing the quality of complaints and enhancing the responsiveness of the law enforcement.
