AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Cybercrime cases are regularly producing hybrid queries that need both the technical evidence and regulatory requirements to be interpreted along with the legal rules that are specific to jurisdiction. Even general-purpose large language models are not reliable in such environment as unsupported legal claims, missing reporting obligations, and lack of traceability of the source can still be present. An architectural design of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)-based legaltech is introduced, to analyse and mitigate cybercrime. The architecture breaks down the workflow into query decomposition, dual-domain legal and cybersecurity retrieval, evidence fusion and reranking, grounded answer generation, verification and escalation, which are overseen by a control-tower orchestration layer. An implementation based on prototyping and a controlled evaluation protocol are defined with definite Indian legal and cybersecurity corpora, queries of a practitioner-style cyber-law and comparative generator settings. In this configuration, the best overall profile corresponds to GPT-5.4, then Claude Sonnet 4.6, DeepSeek-V3.2 and Perplexity Sonar is considered an external grounded-product baseline. The pattern of results suggests that the main benefits are generated by the workflow control, evidence grounding, and verification as opposed to the unconstrained generation alone. The article presents a modular cyber-legal RAG architecture, an experimenter-congruent experimental design, and a blueprint of deployment towards grounded legal AI in cybercrime-related processes.
