AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This Knowledge Brief examines how technology-facilitated gender-based violence (TFGBV) is emerging as a critical barrier to inclusive digital transformation in the Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan, and Pakistan. It shows that while digital tools can expand women’s access to economic opportunities, online harms such as surveillance, coercion, image-based abuse, deepfakes, harassment, and gendered disinformation can push women out of digital spaces and reinforce existing constraints on their participation. The brief argues that TFGBV is not only a safety issue but also an economic and development challenge, with consequences for women’s labor market entry, digital entrepreneurship, civic engagement, access to services, and psychological well-being—especially in contexts shaped by restrictive gender norms, conflict, fragility, and weak legal protection. It calls for stronger legal and institutional frameworks, platform accountability, survivor-centered reporting and support systems, privacy- and safety-by-design approaches, and prevention measures that embed women’s digital safety into digital economy, service delivery, education, and social protection strategies.
