DO  - https://doi.org/10.63665/temj.v02i07.510
AB  - [SGMAY: ethics essay] In the algorithm age, where cybercrimes proliferate through AI-driven harms like deepfakes, algorithmic bias, and data breaches, ancient Indian principles of ahimsa (nonviolence) and karma (causal ethics) offer a profound framework for prevention and remediation. Revived from texts such as the Bhagavad Gita, Upanishads, and Arthashastra, these doctrines reframe digital violence as "severance of vitalities" encompassing not just physical injury but virtual erosions of agency, autonomy, and dignity. This paper explores ahimsa's legal transposition into cyberspace, advocating algorithms imbued with non-harm imperatives to mitigate discriminatory outcomes under India's Article 14 (equality) and Article 21 (dignity, as per Puttaswamy judgment). Karma's retributive logic underpins remedial mechanisms, aligning perpetrator traceability with IT Act 2000 provisions and emerging cyberlaw reforms, while rejecting Big Tech immunities akin to U.S. Section 230. By integrating Jain definitions of himsa with Maslow's needs hierarchy, we propose sovereign ethical audits for AI systems in smart cities and e-governance, fostering dharma-compliant designs that prioritize self-preservation and collective harmony. Such a synthesis empowers Dharmic communities to shape global standards, transcending Western utilitarianism for culturally resonant jurisprudence that curbs remote harms in autonomous vehicles and national IDs. Ultimately, embedding ahimsa-karma in code ensures karmic accountability, transforming punitive cyberlaws into proactive shields against digital adharma.
