AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This chapter provides a comparative analysis of national frameworks addressing AI-driven cybercrime across the European Union, United States, United Kingdom, Middle East, and Asia. It contrasts the EU's layered, risk-based regulatory model—integrating data protection, AI classification, and cybersecurity directives—with the United States' sector-specific and largely reactive enforcement approach. The UK's Online Safety regime illustrates a duty-of-care model emphasizing safety-by-design and ethical oversight, while Middle Eastern strategies, particularly in Saudi Arabia and the UAE, intertwine AI deployment with centralized state security priorities. In Asia, India and South Korea demonstrate hybrid models blending legacy cybercrime statutes with emerging AI governance mechanisms. The chapter highlights divergences in legal definitions, enforcement philosophies, and ethical safeguards, showing how regulatory variation affects compliance costs, innovation incentives, geopolitical alignment, and cross-border cooperation.
