AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Abstract: In the era of Artificial Intelligence ‘deepfake technology’ is one of the most vulnerable software; by using this Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) software, the wrongdoer can convert or replace the original images and audio into a fake one. This technology gaining popularity from 20th century, sometime provides some benefits in various sectors in education, customer service, public awareness etc. Due to deepfake technology, a wrong message circulates in the air, and also various kinds of threats come forth, e.g., cyber defamation, harassment, blackmailing, fulfilling political agendas, threats to national securities, etc. The Ministry of Electronics & IT (MeitY) has been taking various regulatory initiatives to address these issues. The IT Act, 2000, DPDPA, 2023, BNS, 2023 covers some portion related about cybercrimes. The Indian Cyber Security Threat Report, 2025, the rapid development of social engineering technology is making it easier to make manipulating content, fake audio and videos. The DFDC dataset is also recognised as a standard dataset for deepfake detection. But till now no fixed laws and sufficient laws regarding this. This worse invention not only violate the constitutional mandates such as right to privacy (Art 21), also hampers human rights of the individuals. In this paper the researcher will address the national framework also do a comprehensive study of some developing nations such as USA, UK and China. This study limited to doctrinal method and aims to contribute some recommendation about the ongoing discourse of misuse especial focus on human rights violation and right to privacy.
