AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid advancement of Artificial Intelligence (AI) has fundamentally transformed the digital ecosystem, creating unprecedented opportunities for innovation while simultaneously facilitating sophisticated forms of cybercrime. Among the emerging technology-enabled offences, AI-driven automated sextortion has become one of the most alarming threats to women and children due to its capacity to generate synthetic intimate content, automate online grooming, manipulate digital identities, and facilitate large-scale cyber-enabled sexual exploitation. Unlike conventional cyberstalking, which relied primarily on repeated online harassment, surveillance, and intimidation through digital communication, AI-powered technologies such as deepfakes, voice cloning, facial recognition, generative artificial intelligence, and automated chatbots have expanded the scope, speed, and impact of cyber-enabled offences. These developments have challenged traditional legal concepts relating to privacy, modesty, consent, dignity, and criminal liability, exposing significant gaps within existing legal and regulatory frameworks. The present study adopts a doctrinal and socio-legal approach to examine the transformation of conventional cyberstalking into AI-driven automated sextortion with particular reference to women and children in India. The paper critically analyses the adequacy of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, the Information Technology Act, 2000, the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012, the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, and other relevant legal instruments in addressing AI-enabled sexual exploitation. It further examines international regulatory developments relating to artificial intelligence governance and platform accountability to identify comparative best practices.
