AU  - Fouzia Amin
AU  - Muhammad Arif
PY  - 2026
JO  - Qlantic journal of social sciences.
DO  - https://doi.org/10.55737/qjss.vii-iv.26590
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The digitalization of Pakistan has outgrown the significance of institutional framework required to safeguard citizens, especially women and gender minorities, against technology-facilitated gender based violence (TFGBV). With greater connectivity, there's been cyber harassment, image-based abuse without consent, and AI-driven deepfake abuse, highlighting a growing divide between digital opportunity and security. Regardless of this, Pakistan has not established a coordinated institutional framework to support the realization of the law for survivors, particularly the Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act (PECA) 2016 and the addition of its amendment in 2025. This article attempts to explore the extent and nature of TFGBV, assess the responses of the Pakistani government and the country's legal and institutional framework, compare it with the other countries including South Korea, Australia, the United Kingdom and the European Union, and recommend TFGBV National Coordination Framework. The study, based on the qualitative documents analysis, comparative case studies and policy analysis, reveals that there is a major shortcoming in the criminal law in Pakistan, which is lack of institutional responsibility. There are seven or more agencies with overlapping and poorly coordinated mandates and a conviction rate of about 3.5 percent, and this compromises survivors' trust. The article advocates a single empowered coordination cell tailored to the institutional and financial environment in Pakistan. Notably, the Ministry of Human Rights and the UNDP’s National Strategy to Address TFGBV (2026-2030) independently align on a similar model of coordination cell.
