AU  - Dayanandappa Kori
PY  - 2026
JO  - The Serials Librarian
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1080/0361526x.2026.2733185
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This study examined the preparedness of Indian LIS professionals to address challenges posed by deepfake technologies. Data were collected via an online questionnaire administered through Google Forms from 401 Library and Information Science professionals in India. The study used descriptive statistics, ANOVA, MANOVA, the Pearson correlation coefficient, and Structural Equation Modelling (SEM). Results indicated that male respondents reported higher scores in deepfake awareness and recognition. Recognition was linked to support for mitigation strategies, including AI-based detection, platform governance, moderation, and media literacy initiatives, and this pattern was consistent across demographic groups. Recognition of deepfakes was positively linked to support for related initiatives. Awareness was positively associated with confidence and understanding of deepfake threats and AI systems. Model fit indices indicated a good fit: RMSEA = 0.051, CFI = 0.972, IFI = 0.972, NFI = 0.947. The study emphasises the importance of awareness campaigns, media literacy, and education in combating deepfake misinformation. Findings add to the discourse on digital literacy and trust in the LIS field, underscoring the need for targeted policies and educational strategies to address deepfake challenges.
