TI  - [SGINC] Cyberbullying in India: 5 Things Parents and Schools Must Do Right Now
AU  - Narayan Rout
PY  - 2026
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22139594
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Cyberbullying prevalence estimates among Indian adolescents vary widely across studies, from roughly 10% in tightly defined clinical research to 85% in broader cross-country surveys, reflecting real differences in methodology rather than a single settled figure. This article works from the most methodologically rigorous recent Indian data, a 2025 meta-analysis pooling ten studies at 19% pooled prevalence and a 2023 Bengaluru clinical study finding 14.5% of school-going adolescents as victims, alongside the UDAYA longitudinal cohort study's documented statistical link between cyberbullying victimisation and depression and suicidal ideation. It lays out India's current legal framework for responding to cyberbullying involving minors: the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023 (which replaced the IPC in July 2024), the Information Technology Act 2000, the POCSO Act 2012's mandatory reporting duty when sexual content is involved, and the Juvenile Justice Act's reform-focused process when the person bullying is also a minor. It presents five concrete, evidence-based actions, two for parents (open communication, since most cyberbullied children in India confide in friends rather than adults; and recognising real behavioural warning signs) and two for schools (enforcing, not merely holding, the anti-cyberbullying policy CBSE and CISCE already require; and training staff to distinguish cyberbullying from ordinary peer conflict), plus a shared responsibility to know India's free, government-backed reporting infrastructure: Childline 1098, the National Cyber Crime Helpline 1930, and the NCPCR's eBaalNidan portal.
