AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Background Cyberbullying has emerged as a major public health and psychological concern in the digital era. Its harmful consequences extend beyond victims and perpetrators, affecting bystanders who witness online aggression. The way bystanders respond whether by helping victims, remaining passive, or supporting the bullying can significantly influence the impact and continuation of cyberbullying. Understanding the factors that shape these responses is therefore essential for developing effective prevention and intervention strategies. Objective This study examined the different forms of bystander behaviour in cyberbullying and explored the demographic, psychological, and family-related factors associated with these behaviours among secondary school students. Methods A cross-sectional survey was conducted among 578 secondary school students in Indore, India. Participants were selected through simple random sampling from four randomly chosen schools. Data were collected using an online questionnaire that assessed demographic characteristics, cyberbullying experiences, bystander behaviour, attitudes toward cyberbullying, parental attachment, and self-esteem. Standardized instruments, including the Revised Inventory of Parent Attachment and the Revised Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale, were used to measure parental attachment and self-esteem. Results The findings indicated that helping or defending victims was the most frequently reported bystander response (34.6%), followed by ignoring cyberbullying incidents (28.0%) and participating in cyberbullying (26.3%). Students who had personally experienced cyberbullying or had witnessed it affecting friends or family members were more likely to support victims, particularly when they reported stronger parental attachment. In contrast, participants who were only aware of cyberbullying involving the general public were more likely to remain passive. Those who admitted engaging in bullying behaviour and expressed favourable attitudes toward cyberbullying were more likely to join or reinforce online bullying. Conclusion The study highlights that bystander behaviour in cyberbullying is shaped by both personal experiences and psychosocial factors, particularly parental attachment. Strengthening positive family relationships and encouraging supportive bystander responses may help reduce passive observation and active participation in cyberbullying. Future research should continue to examine the mechanisms influencing bystander decisions to support the development of effective prevention and intervention programmes.
