AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Seerie is a scam-prevention assistant for India that runs offline on an Android phone: a QLoRA fine-tune of Qwen3-1.7B shipped as a 1.11 GB 4-bit GGUF file. This is the third model, trained on 140,813 conversations (corpus v407) after every law-section citation, unverified phone number or website and 1,319 corrupted records were removed from training and native Hindi was added. Every answer was read and rated individually by an AI assistant (Claude); the author did not re-rate them. The author’s own manual review will be done for the next version (v408). Note: the PDF’s Acknowledgements state that the author reviewed every rating; that is not correct for this version. On the 100-prompt suite of the previous report the model is safe on 86 answers (previous model 87, untuned base model 23); on the 70 prompts of the first report the three Seerie models are safe on 58, 59 and 57. Cleaning the data removed specific errors but not the error rate: the model still invents law sections although no training record cites one. A fact checker raises agreement between the automatic score and the rating from Cohen’s kappa 0.19 to 0.41. On template-built held-out topics the model passes 408 of 408 checks, but 271 answers copy a training reply word for word. On 84 new hand-written prompts (252 answers) it is safe on 76%: 83% in English, 81% in Hinglish and 52% in Hindi, where most answers were cut off by the test’s token limit. It refused every English request to write scam content, but treats some genuine bank procedures as scams and gave “safety tips” to a described money-mule activity. This record contains the paper (PDF and LaTeX source), the on-device model (seerie-v407-q4_k_m.gguf, 1.11 GB), all 452 rated answers, the raw evaluation outputs, the scripts that recompute every number, and the figures. See README.md for intended use and limitations. The model is also on Hugging Face: https://huggingface.co/seerror-technologies/seerie-v407-GGUF Seerie is a defensive tool. Its answers are not legal, financial or medical advice; in India, report cyber fraud to the national helpline 1930 or cybercrime.gov.in.
