PY  - 2026
JO  - INTERANTIONAL JOURNAL OF SCIENTIFIC RESEARCH IN ENGINEERING AND MANAGEMENT
DO  - https://doi.org/10.55041/ijsrem68798
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Corporate fraud, financial misrepresentation, conflicts of interest and abuse of managerial authority pose persistent challenges to corporate governance and sustainable development in India. Although the Indian legal framework provides for directors’This study focuses duties, audit oversight, disclosure obligations and regulatory supervision, recurring governance failures demonstrate that formal compliance alone cannot secure ethical corporate conduct. This study examines the relevance of the Bhagavad Gita to the ethical and psychological dimensions of corporate misconduct, with particular reference to Shad-Ripu : the six internal enemies traditionally identified as kama (desire), krodha (anger), lobha (greed), moha (attachment or delusion), mada (arrogance) and matsarya (envy). It distinguishes the Gita’s explicit identification of desire, anger and greed in Chapter 16, Verse 21 from the broader Shad-Ripu framework found in Indian ethical thought. The study adopts doctrinal, analytical and comparative methods to examine the Companies Act, 2013, particularly Sections 166, 177 and 135, the SEBI (Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements) Regulations, 2015, and selected corporate governance failures, including Satyam and IL&FS. It argues that philosophical concepts such as dharma, nishkama karma, self-discipline and lokasangraha can enrich the ethical understanding of fiduciary responsibility, without replacing enforceable legal duties. The study proposes a GITA-based Corporate Ethical Governance Model - Good Governance, Integrity, Trustee Responsibility and Accountability, as a conceptual framework for strengthening institutional ethics. It concludes that sustainable corporate governance requires the combined operation of effective law, independent oversight and an organizational culture of ethical responsibility.
