AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Purpose: The rapid expansion of digital technologies has increased women’s participation in online environments while concurrently escalating their exposure to cyber harassment, privacy infringements, and digital financial fraud. This study examines the relationship between Digital Vulnerability and Women’s Human Rights Protection, with particular focus on the mediating role of Institutional Protection among women in the Lucknow region of Uttar Pradesh, India. Design/Methodology/Approach: A quantitative, cross-sectional survey design was executed using a structured questionnaire comprising 15 reflective indicators across three core latent constructs: Digital Vulnerability (predictor), Institutional Protection (mediator), and Women’s Human Rights Protection (outcome). The conceptual framework and hypotheses were evaluated using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM) to perform measurement validation and structural path mediation assessments. Findings: The measurement model exhibited robust indicator reliability (loadings > 0.70), internal consistency, convergent validity (), and discriminant validity. Structural path modeling indicated that Digital Vulnerability significantly predicts Institutional Protection (, ), which in turn significantly predicts Women’s Human Rights Protection (, ). A significant direct association was also observed between Digital Vulnerability and Women’s Human Rights Protection (, ). The indirect mediation path was statistically significant (, ), confirming partial complementary mediation and accounting for an overall total effect of (). The model explained 55.8% () of the variance in Institutional Protection and 28.7% () in Women’s Human Rights Protection. Practical & Social Implications: The results emphasize that addressing technology-facilitated violence requires more than technical solutions; institutional mechanisms—such as legal aid, responsive policing, and accessible reporting channels—are vital intermediary conduits to safeguard women's privacy, dignity, autonomy, and security in digital spaces. Originality: While previous literature predominantly addresses cybercrime prevalence or legal provisions conceptually, this study provides empirical, region-specific evidence testing an integrated structural equation model that confirms institutional protection as a critical mediating mechanism in digital human rights protection.
