TI  - [SGINC] Online Harassment and the Culture of Silence: A Qualitative Study of University Students' Experiences
AU  - Rabia Afzal
AU  - Dr. Waqas Ali Khan
AU  - Khansa Afzal
PY  - 2026
JO  - Journal of international law & human rights.
DO  - https://doi.org/10.59075/ilhr.v5i7.263
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] A significant portion of university students are being harassed online, but many have not reported the harasment. This silence may be reinforced in Pakistan by a lack of trust in institutions, fear of blame and family honour, which do not seem to have been explored through students' perspectives in a qualitative study. The purpose of the present study was to (1) to discuss the experienced harassment and impact of online harassment on emotional health, social interactions and academic work in university students' lives. (2) To explore why students stay silent or fail to report online harassment, in the context of social, cultural and institutional factors. (3) To gain insight into how students cope and what certain kind of support universities and institutions should be providing. A qualitative design was used. The participants were selected using purposive sampling with 18 female students who faced online harassment. Thematic analysis was used to analyse the data. Three themes emerged. The first, lived experience and impact of online harassment, was characterized by repeated abuse via messages, fake accounts and mockery in class discussion groups, resulting in fear, shame, lack of confidence, social isolation from online platforms and academic challenges. This latter, culture of silence and barriers to reporting, revealed that the silence became a product of fear of blame, family honour, distrust of university and legal systems, and the normalisation of harassment. The third, coping skills and anticipated support, demonstrated that students used primarily blocking and privacy settings and some trusted confidants and requested confidential reporting, counselling, trained staff and awareness programmes. The stifling of silence in regards to online harassment is not due to an individual's shortcomings, but due to social and institutional pressures. Universities should have clear, confidential and gender-sensitive reporting and support systems and victim-blaming should be challenged in families and communities.
