ID  - R1068
TI  - [SGINC] FROM SADVRITTA TO CYBER RESPONSIBILITY: ANCIENT INDIAN ETHICAL FRAMEWORKS FOR COMBATING CYBERBULLYING, MISINFORMATION, AND DIGITAL FRAUD
AU  - Saumya Shukla
AU  - Neelam Mishra
PY  - 2026
JO  - Transformative Education A Multidisciplinary Journal
DO  - https://doi.org/10.63665/temj.v02i07.515
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid expansion of digital technologies has transformed communication, commerce, and governance, but it has also generated new forms of misconduct such as cyberbullying, misinformation, identity theft, and online fraud. Contemporary legal frameworks attempt to regulate these challenges through cyber laws and regulatory mechanisms; however, legal deterrence alone often proves insufficient in addressing the ethical dimensions of digital behaviour. This paper explores the relevance of Indian Knowledge Systems (IKS), particularly the Ayurvedic concept of Sadvritta (ethical conduct), as a normative ethical framework for promoting responsible conduct in cyberspace. Rooted in classical Ayurvedic texts, Sadvritta represents a comprehensive code of moral, social, and mental discipline that guides individuals toward virtuous behaviour and harmonious social relations. The study examines the five dimensions of Sadvritta—Vyavaharika (ethical conduct), Samajika (social conduct), Manasika (mental discipline), Dharmika (moral righteousness), and Sharirika (physical discipline)—and analyzes their potential application to contemporary digital environments. From a legal perspective, these principles can serve as a behavioural framework that complements cyber laws by fostering self-regulation, ethical awareness, and digital responsibility among users. For instance, values such as truthfulness (Satya), non-violence (Ahimsa), and compassion (Daya) can help counter online harassment, digital fraud, and the spread of misinformation, thereby strengthening ethical digital citizenship. By situating Sadvritta within the broader discourse on Traditional Knowledge and Sustainable Development, this paper argues that integrating ancient ethical principles into modern cyber governance can contribute to achieving the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly SDG 16, which emphasizes peace, justice, and strong institutions. The paper adopts a doctrinal and interdisciplinary approach, drawing from Ayurvedic texts, contemporary legal scholarship, and cyber ethics literature. Ultimately, it proposes that the integration of traditional ethical frameworks with modern cyber law can create a more holistic approach to addressing digital crimes, promoting responsible online behaviour, and building a sustainable and ethically grounded digital society.
