AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The Aadhaar Enabled Payment System (AePS) has been a transformative force in India's financial inclusion mission, enabling millions of unbanked and underbanked citizens to access banking services through biometric authentication. However, this convenience has created a rapidly expanding attack surface for cybercriminals. According to the Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C), AePS-related fraud accounted for 11% of all online financial frauds in India during 2023 [1]. In 2024, cyber fraud losses across India surged to ₹22,845 crore — a staggering 206% increase from 2023 [14]. This paper presents a comprehensive investigation into the threat taxonomy of AePS fraud, examining biometric cloning, data breaches, malicious Business Correspondent (BC) agent activities, and social engineering attacks. The study evaluates the regulatory responses of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), NPCI, and UIDAI, and proposes a multi-layered prevention framework combining technical, behavioural, and policy-level interventions.
