AU  - Sahana Sarkar
PY  - 2026
JO  - Routledge eBooks
DO  - https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003587316-2
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This chapter examines the intersection of gender, technology, and violence, situating TFSV within long-standing structures of gendered power rather than treating it as a novel digital phenomenon. Drawing on feminist and sociological scholarship, it traces the historical association between masculinity and technology, showing how digital spaces simultaneously enable women’s participation and reproduce patriarchal control. The chapter reviews key manifestations of TFSV, including e-bile, image-based sexual abuse, doxxing, and cyberstalking, using continuum of sexual violence to demonstrate how these harms are interconnected, cumulative, and embedded within broader systems of gendered oppression. Particular attention is given to the Indian context, where research remains comparatively limited despite the widespread prevalence of TFSV, and to violence perpetrated by unknown persons, an underexamined area relative to intimate partner abuse. The chapter argues that stranger-perpetrated TFSV is not random but structured by intersecting hierarchies of caste, religion, sexuality, and disability. It also considers the psychological, social, and physical impacts of TFSV, alongside women’s strategies of resistance and risk negotiation, including withdrawal, platform tools, and collective action. In establishing this theoretical and empirical foundation, the chapter identifies critical gaps in Global South scholarship and positions the book’s central contribution: an intersectional examination of Indian women’s experiences of TFSV by unknown perpetrators.
