TI  - [SGINC] Digital Deception: A Feminist Jurisprudential Critique of Technology Facilitated Violence against Women
AU  - Shashwat Kabir
PY  - 2026
JO  - International Journal of Versatile Research and Analysis
DO  - https://doi.org/10.56975/ijvra.v4i9.710735
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid rise of generative artificial intelligence has transformed the cybercrime landscape, facilitating advanced manifestations of Technology-Facilitated Gender-Based Violence (TFGBV) like non-consensual deepfakes and algorithmic identity fraud. Traditional penal frameworks and cyber laws, based on paradigms of physical or economic harm, do not account for the grave psychological and reputational trauma inflicted by these technologies. In this paper, the author analyzes the structural inadequacies of the existing legal framework in India namely the Information Technology Act and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS) from a feminist jurisprudential lens. It contends that framing deepfake abuse within traditional obscenity or defamation laws fails to capture the gendered violence and deep violation of ‘intimate privacy’. Also, the paper highlights huge forensic bottlenecks placing insurmountable evidentiary burden on victims under Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam (BSA). Drawing upon comparative international approaches such as the European Union’s AI Act and mandatory provenance tracking, it suggests the articulation of a new legal standard of “digital dignity.” It calls for a separate TFGBV law, swift injunctive relief, and a change in the rules of evidence to cryptographic provenance in an effort to create a fairer digital legal system.
