AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Cyberstalking is that adapted form of interpersonal violence that is not just mere harassment but a fusion of intrusive, recurring, and trailing behaviors that creates a tenacious sense of threat to the target. The study came forward with the aspiration to examine how university students perceive the risk of cyberstalking and the emotional and psychological distress associated with it. The data was gathered through a semi-structured interview and was analyzed following Braun and Clarke’s (2006) six-framework thematic analysis approach. Cyberstalking is a perceived widespread and normalized phenomenon. The study reported reliance on avoidance-based coping strategies, limitations of formal reporting, observation of a gap in digital competence, awareness of cyber safety practices; vitality to enhanced awareness, institutional support, and strategies to address cyberstalking and its consequences. The evidence demonstrated that cyberstalking is accelerated by anonymity, exorbitant social media use, and technological ignorance or incompetence, with substantial emotional and psychological consequences, including fear, anxiety, stress, and depression which influenced their academic performance and daily functioning reliance on avoidance-based coping strategies, such as ignoring and blocking perpetrators; limitations of formal reporting attributable to lack of trust in institutions and fear of stigma; observation of a gap in digital competence and awareness of cyber safety practices, with benefaction to the existing literature and in-depth qualitative comprehension of cyberstalking perceptions within the Pakistani context.
