AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Background: The rapid expansion of internet accessibility and smartphone ownership hastransformed how adolescents communicate, exposing them to an increasing risk ofcyberbullying. Cyberbullying — repeated harassment, humiliation, or threatening behaviourconducted through electronic media — has emerged as a significant adolescent public healthconcern in India and globally. Objective: This review synthesizes current evidence on themagnitude, psychological consequences, and public health implications of cyberbullyingamong adolescents, and proposes a conceptual framework for prevention. Methods: A narrativereview of peer-reviewed literature and institutional reports (World Health Organization,UNICEF, UNESCO) published up to 2024 was conducted using electronic databases, focusingon studies addressing cyberbullying prevalence, psychosocial impact, and preventiveinterventions among adolescents. Findings: Global prevalence of adolescent cyberbullyingranges from 15–35%, with Indian studies reporting 20–40%. Victims commonly experienceanxiety, depression, low self-esteem, sleep disturbance, and academic decline, with nearly twice the risk of depressive symptoms and suicidal ideation compared with non-victims.Underreporting, inadequate digital literacy, and limited school-based mental health servicesdelay identification and intervention. Conclusion: Cyberbullying requires a coordinated,multidisciplinary public health response. The proposed Cyber SHIELD Model —encompassing screen monitoring, healthy digital literacy, identification of warning signs,emotional support, legal awareness, and digital wellness counseling — offers a structuredframework for schools, families, and health professionals to prevent cyberbullying and promoteadolescent digital well-being.
