AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] A significant growth of social media sites, online forums, and electronic means of communication has seen an increase in cyber stalking, virtual sexual harassment, and cyber violence. While the digital revolution has changed the way in which communication is conducted, the way in which communication is conducted, the way in which education is imparted, business is conducted, and the way people interact with one another, this revolution has made women and children more susceptible to cybercrimes like cyberbullying, identity theft, revenge porn, morphing, sextortion, impersonation, and online harassment. The latest statistics from the NCRB reveal an alarming number of cybercrimes.Artificial Intelligence (AI) has proven to be an essential tool when dealing with cybercrimes committed against women as well as ensuring cybersecurity. Content moderation tools that make use of AI can detect abusive content, including messages, pictures, and comments, thus providing a more secure virtual space. NLP (Natural Language Processing), machine learning, and computer vision can be used in the detection of cyberstalking and suspicious online activity, as well as for identifying explicit content. Yet, there are numerous challenges in implementing AI technologies for tackling cybercrimes, including legal, ethical, and technical issues. First, AI needs considerable amounts of information, thereby posing risks to users’ privacy. Second, a flawed training data set could lead to biased decision making about online harassment. This paper provides an analysis of the application of AI to cyberstalking and sexual harassment of women in cyberspace in India by means of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita and Information Technology Act. However, in addition to innovation, effective legislation and awareness are crucial.
