AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Introduction: Cyberbullying has emerged as a significant psychosocial issue among students due to the rapid expansion of digital communication and social media use. Nursing students, who frequently engage in online academic and social platforms, may be particularly vulnerable to cyberbullying. Exposure to cyberbullying can negatively affect students’ psychological well-being, academic performance, and professional development. Despite its growing relevance, limited research has explored the prevalence of cyberbullying specifically among nursing students in the Indian context. This study was undertaken to assess the prevalence of cyberbullying among nursing students in a selected nursing school of Greater Noida, Uttar Pradesh. Methods: A quantitative, descriptive cross-sectional research design was adopted for the study. The sample consisted of nursing students selected using a non-probability sampling technique. Data were collected using a structured and validated cyberbullying assessment questionnaire that measured experiences of cyberbullying victimization and perpetration. Demographic variables such as age, gender, year of study, residence, and internet usage patterns were also included. Ethical approval was obtained, and informed consent was taken from all participants. Data were analysed using descriptive statistics to determine the prevalence of cyberbullying. Results: The findings revealed that a notable proportion of nursing students had experienced cyberbullying in various forms, including online harassment, spreading of rumours, and offensive messages. Cyberbullying prevalence was found to vary across demographic characteristics, with higher exposure reported among students with increased social media usage and prolonged internet engagement. The results indicate that cyberbullying is a prevalent concern among nursing students. Discussion: The study highlights cyberbullying as an important issue in nursing education that requires immediate attention. Awareness programs, institutional policies, and supportive reporting mechanisms are essential to address and prevent cyberbullying. Early identification and preventive strategies can help promote a safe learning environment and enhance students’ mental well-being and professional growth.
