AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The emergence of deepfake technology—synthetic media generated through artificial intelligence—has created unprecedented challenges for cybercrime prosecution. Deepfakes can manipulate audio, video, and images to produce highly realistic yet falsified content, threatening personal reputation, financial security, and national stability. This paper examines the legal and evidentiary challenges posed by deepfakes, evaluates current legislative frameworks in India, the United States, and the European Union, and identifies gaps in prosecutorial capacity and judicial readiness. Through comparative analysis, case studies, and expert commentary, the study highlights the need for robust evidentiary standards, AI-assisted forensic tools, and comprehensive legislative reform. Recommendations emphasize balancing technological innovation, privacy rights, and the integrity of criminal justice systems.
