AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid development of digital technologies and artificial intelligence has transformed the manner in which women communicate, work, learn and participate in public life. At the same time, digital spaces have created new forms of gender-based violence, including cyberstalking, doxing, non-consensual dissemination of intimate images, identity theft, online sexual harassment and AI-generated deep fakes. These practices can seriously affect women’s privacy, dignity, reputation, autonomy and freedom of expression. The problem is particularly significant because digital content can be copied, manipulated and circulated globally within seconds. In India, constitutional protection of privacy under Article 21, together with the Information Technology Act, 2000, the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 and the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, provides several legal mechanisms for addressing technology-related harms. However, the rapid development of generative artificial intelligence creates challenges that existing laws were not specifically designed to address. This article examines women’s digital rights in the context of privacy, cyberstalking, deep fakes and online harassment and suggests legal, technological and institutional measures for strengthening protection.
