JO  - ACCENT JOURNAL OF  ECONOMICS ECOLOGY  & ENGINEERING
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rapid expansion of the internet has facilitated the rise of the dark web—a hidden, anonymous layer of the internet accessed via tools like Tor—where illegal activities such as drug trafficking, identity theft, financial fraud, and child exploitation thrive. In India, while digitization has progressed rapidly, legal and enforcement mechanisms have struggled to keep up with the anonymity and technological sophistication of dark web-related cybercrime. This research critically explores the legal and criminological challenges posed by the dark web in the Indian context. It examines the effectiveness of key laws, including the Information Technology Act, 2000 and the Indian Penal Code, and identifies gaps in enforcement, forensic capabilities, and judicial readiness. Criminological theories like Routine Activity Theory and Differential Association are applied to analyze offender behavior and motivations. A comparative study with frameworks in the United States and European Union reveals best practices that India could adopt. The paper concludes with recommendations such as legal reforms, enhanced investigative capacity, international cooperation, and increased public cyber-awareness. Ultimately, it advocates for a multi-stakeholder, interdisciplinary approach to bolster India’s resilience against dark web cyber threats and improve cyber governance in the digital era
