AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] With the advent of widespread internet usage, crimes such as cyberstalking, harassment, and identity theft have escalated, disproportionately affecting women. The current research uses text mining methods to analyze big data and establish trends and patterns of cybercrime in different cities of India. Using research methods such as equality testing-Principal Component Analysis (PCA), as well as heatmaps, the work reveals the distribution and characteristics of the studied crimes. The paper also measures the performance of several machine learning techniques: Random Forest, Support Vector Machine (SVM), and K- Nearest Neighbors (KNN) to assess high risk and low risk cities of cybercrime. These results reinforce the fact that countries should develop far-superior methods to solve the cybercrime problem, particularly concerning violence against women on social media websites. The study also outlines research recommendations for future studies as follows; incorporation of real-time data obtained from the social media platforms and strengthening the partnership with law enforcement to come up with better strategies of combating cybercrimes to protect women within the digital environment.
