AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] In the era of the Internet where food and cab rides are only a click away, so is love! Dating today has undergone a significant facelift, courtesy of technological advancements, transforming the age-old dating scenario into a market for users to virtually shop for partners. Consequently, AI has become the new matchmaker for scoring dates, which companies find much more lucrative to invest in considering “finding a romantic partner is one of the biggest problems that humans face”. Whilst there is no denying that these websites and apps carry the potential to bring about happiness to the users, many do not recognise the associated inevitable risks that may come forth, whilst love may or may not. Online fraud, sexual violence, impersonation, cyberstalking, etc., are only a few grains of sand in the sea of cybercrimes associated with such platforms [1]. Through this research, the authors intend to analyse the connection between online dating websites and cybercrimes, focusing particularly on the pornography aspect of online dating, which occurs via hacking (first stage), developing into sextortion, and may even lead to online prostitution, and evaluate whether the pros outweigh the cons. This paper will also analyse the legal and regulatory framework in India and foreign jurisdictions to curb the menace associated with online dating and suggest legal interventions and strategies to help users and the government mitigate these potential harms so that the users have the experience and possibly love they intend to find.
