TI  - [SGINC] Deepfake Technology and Criminal Liability Legal Challenges in the Digital Age
AU  - Imtiaz Hussain
AU  - Sajid Sultan
AU  - Muhammad Faiq Butt
PY  - 2025
JO  - Journal for Current Sign
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] With the fast growing of AI-powered solutions, the creation process of AV assets has begun to change with technologies that can create high-quality real-looking images, voices and videos of people. Deepfakes are one of its most important expressions since they can create seemingly real images of people, things and says. Legitimate uses are entertainment, education, accessibility and the creative industries, while its malicious use presents serious challenges to the criminal law. Deepfakes can be used to create and use fake identities for the purposes of identity theft, fraud, extortion, defamation, non-consensual intimate imagery, political manipulation, harassment and fabrication of digital evidence. The main legal challenge is that traditional criminal law considers the acts of people in liability relationships, these acts being linked via relatively identifiable causal chains but in the case of deepfake production, users, developers, platforms, automated systems and anonymous intermediaries can act across different jurisdictions in liability relationships. This article considers how deepfake technology relates to actus reus, mens rea, and criminal responsibility along with three other issues, namely: intermediary responsibility, evidentiary reliability, and jurisdiction, which seek to protect the victim. It makes a comparative study of emerging approaches of European Union, United Kingdom, United States and Pakistan. The article calls into question the statefulness of criminalization as an adequate response. Individual punishments should be combined with platforms obligations, evidentiary protection, provenance systems, victim measures and digital-capacity protection in a technologically neutral, but deepfake-sensitive law. Pakistan especially needs a more refined system under legislation that can differentiate if the misinformation is an innocent gesture and not with the intent of creating or manipulating technology to cause harm to a particular identity whilst safeguard the constitutional freedoms of expression and due process.
