AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The exponential growth of India’s digital ecosystem has been accompanied by a surge in cybercrime, including identity theft, online fraud, and cyberstalking. Despite legislative provisions under the Information Technology Act, 2000, and relevant amendments to the Indian Penal Code, conviction rates in cybercrime cases remain disproportionately low. One of the principal factors contributing to this gap is judicial delay. This study examines the socio-legal implications of judicial delays in cybercrime cases in India, with emphasis on their impact on conviction rates, economic costs, and public trust in the justice system. Using a quantitative, socio-legal design, the study analyzes data drawn from the National Judicial Data Grid (NJDG), National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB), and select High Courts between 2015–2024. Hypotheses are tested through statistical models to assess correlations between judicial delay and conviction rates, economic losses, and efficiency gains from technologica l adoption. The findings are expected to inform reforms in judicial processes, policy frameworks, and adoption of digital technologies for expedited adjudication. By bridging cybersecurity concerns with judicial efficiency, the study highlights the need for a resilient legal system capable of addressing the evolving complexities of cybercrime.
