AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Due to the emergence of cybercrime and the use of electronically stored information during criminal trials, the role of digital forensics in Pakistan has taken on a more critical nature. Although there are such legislative measures as the Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act (PECA) 2016 and the clauses of the Qanun-e-Shahadat Order (1984), troubles related to the admissibility, authenticity, and reliability of digital evidence have remained. The literature provides evidence of the lack of procedural safeguards, ineffective chain of custody, technical deficiency, and inadequate forensic infrastructure, as all of them weaken the right to a fair trial according to Article 10-A of the Constitution. Privacy concerns, cybersecurity, and court inconsistencies also contribute to the matter of digital forensics adaptation to the justice system being even more challenging. The lessons on best practices in international practices teach that procedural changes, special training, and accredited forensic laboratories are direly needed in Pakistan. Digital forensic capacity could be improved to make the process more objective and develop the credibility of prosecutions of cybercriminals, as well as make the legislation more consistent with international norms.
