TY  - JOUR
ID  - R1255
TI  - [SGINC] Legal Framework Against Cyber Bullying In India: A Comparative Study With Usa
AU  - Harshit Harshit
PY  - 2025
JO  - International Journal of Research Publication and Reviews
DO  - https://doi.org/10.55248/gengpi.06.1125.38172
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] As a repeated pattern of offensive, humiliating, or intimidating communication through digital means, cyberbullying defies the classification of traditional criminal laws and causes problems with intermediary liability, school discipline, and constitutional speech rights.The doctrinal question in India is essentially about the use of composite "Section 66C", "Section 66D", "Section 66E", "Section 67", "Section 67A" and "Section 67B of the Information Technology Act, 2000" together with the specific offences in the "Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023" and the "Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023" and "Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023" procedural ways to deal with constant online harassment of reputation, privacy, autonomy, and psychological integrity.The framework is also interwoven with the child protection provisions under the "Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012" and with the upcoming data protection provisions under the "Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023", especially "Section 9", which elevates the duties in respect of children's data as a source of bullying, shaming, and doxxing.Meanwhile, the "Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021" provide for the graded due diligence of intermediaries, such as the notice-and-takedown work, and traceability and compliance mechanisms for the major social media entities that create a quasi-regulatory framework in which content governance becomes central to harm mitigation.123 A comparison with the United States through a comparative lens reveals a unique structural difference.The United States places intermediary immunity most prominently in "Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act", which keeps a wide protection for platforms against liability for third-party content, while regulation of cyberbullying mainly comes from state anti-bullying legislations, school district codes, and student-speech case law governed by the First Amendment.This difference shows how India's offence-centric approach and intermediaries' due diligence duties are quite different from the American model which protects platforms and directs the regulatory pressure to educational institutions and civil remedies.The study contends that regulation of cyberbullying should not be dependent only on criminalization or platform immunity; it must include calibrated norms on repeated digital harassment, procedural standards for complaint handling and digital forensics, and harmonized duties for schools, intermediaries, police, and courts.By means of doctrinal and comparative analysis, the paper examines if the current Indian combination of "IT Act, 2000", "IT Rules 2021", "BNS", "BNSS", "BSA", and "DPDP Act, 2023" provides a consistent and proportional response to cyberbullying or whether there is a need for structural reforms that are informed by and deliberately different from the U.S. experience in order to ensure dignity, freedom of expression, and due process in online spaces.
N1  - R1255 | tier B
