TY  - JOUR
ID  - R1264
TI  - [SGINC] Linking Sadism with Workplace Cyberbullying Perpetration: Psychological Well-being as a Mediator
AU  - Arooj Khan
AU  - Omer Farooq Malik
AU  - Asif Shahzad
PY  - 2025
JO  - International Journal of Business and Management Sciences
DO  - https://doi.org/10.63468/ijbm.6.4.09
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Workplace cyberbullying (WCB) perpetration is the technology-mediated behavior to humiliate or harass coworkers in the work context, thereby becoming a matter of concern for organizations because of more dependency on information and communication technologies. Given its probable predictors, the personality trait of sadism is a significant factor that provokes cyberbullying behavior, since sadistic personality traits are more prone to the characteristics of a cyberbully, which calls for attention in the context of the workplace. This study addressed the research call by examining the direct as well as the indirect association between sadism and WCB perpetration by analyzing psychological well-being as the mediator. This link was established on the theoretical tenets of the General Aggression Model (GAM). Data were collected from 355 valid respondents, including both male and female faculty members from universities affiliated with HEC, Pakistan. The Structural Equation Modeling (CB-SEM) was performed in AMOS software to evaluate the measurement model and the structural model, and to test the hypotheses. The statistical results are in support of the model by establishing a direct and indirect association between sadism and WCB perpetration. The present investigation extends the scope of antecedents to the existing literature of WCB by demonstrating that the personality trait of sadism leads towards aggressive behavior of WCB perpetration. Further, this study tested a novel model by incorporating psychological well-being as the mediator between personality and behavior towards WCB perpetration, based on GAM. The theoretical implications and practical implications of this research are also discussed.
