TI  - [SGINC] REGULATING DEEPFAKE TECHNOLOGY AND ITS USE IN CHILD SEXUAL ABUSE MATERIAL UNDER INDIAN LAW
AU  - Pallvi Pallvi
AU  - Vinod Kumar Sharma
PY  - 2025
JO  - International Journal of Research Publication and Reviews
DO  - https://doi.org/10.55248/gengpi.06.1125.38125
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Regulating AI-generated child sexual abuse material is a highly challenging doctrinal and enforcement issue in India.The main reason is that the existing rules under the "Information Technology Act, 2000", "Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012", "Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023" and "Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021" were made with the assumption of real world or traditional digital images in mind.They do not cater for synthetic outputs which may show children but not necessarily require a real child for the production of the content."Section 67B of the IT Act, 2000" already makes it illegal any publication, transmission, creation, collection or browsing of material that depicts children in a sexually explicit or indecent manner in any electronic form.It is also quite broad in its textual coverage so as to include AI-generated CSAM if it depicts minors visually.The pornographic provisions in "Sections 13, 14, and 15 of POCSO" also talk about the use of a child for pornographic purposes, simulated sexual acts, and storage of such material which could be straightforwardly interpreted to extend to synthetic or morphed images that depict a real or apparently underage child.However, enforcement agencies, intermediaries, and courts still grapple with the problem of classifying deepfakes that, among other things, merge the real faces of minors with synthetic bodies for non-existent minors, use anime-style avatars, or age-modify adult images and figuring out the attribution of mens rea in content production chains that are decentralized.The safe harbor regime under "Section 79 of the IT Act, 2000" and the due diligence requirements in the "IT Rules, 2021" and subsequent amendments put heavy burdens on intermediaries to take action against CSAM and employ automated tools.Yet, the processes for proactive detection, cross-platform hash sharing, traceability, and quick removal of deepfake CSAM are still disjointed.Definitional clarity gaps, lack of explicit recognition of AI synthetic CSAM in the statute, limited victim-centric remedies, and weak accountability for non-compliant platforms are some of the reasons children are exposed to continuous harm.This research fills that void by comprehensively mapping the existing framework and suggesting appropriate legislative changes, intermediary duties, and digital evidence protocols that are in line with Indian statutory design, executive advisories on deepfakes, and the emerging judicial directions on sexual imagery and personality rights.
N1  - R1277 | tier B
