AU  - Siri Vadlamudi
PY  - 2025
DO  - https://doi.org/10.65694/icgst2025-cp01-003
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Currently, India ranks tenth place in the World Cybercrime Index among nearly 100 countries with the average number of cases rising to 7,000 daily in 2023. Criminals posing as law enforcement or officials to frighten individuals into surrendering funds or private details forms the core of the Digital Arrest Scam, a sophisticated cybercrime originating in and specific to India. However, the seriousness and scale of Digital Arrest has not translated into adequate research with the phrase lacking an official place in academic taxonomies of cybercrime. The purpose of this study is to bridge the gap in research on Digital Arrest Scams by investigating the experience of victims in tandem with available quantitative data. It will test hypotheses concerning the influence of sociodemographic factors on financial loss and fear levels. To do this, ten detailed semi-structured interviews were taken with victims of this scam. Secondary data was gathered from academic papers and official reports to contextualize these findings. The results showed that education level, gender, occupation and Aadhaar linkage impacted fear and financial loss. Furthermore, no participant contacted police and even thought that cybersecurity helplines did not make them feel reassured. In conclusion, the findings reveal that demographic factors do indeed have a significant effect on victim vulnerability, allowing us to define the vulnerability profile. This highlights the need for targeted digital literacy programs and improved cybersecurity response.
