AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Cybercrime has emerged as a significant global threat, transcending borders and challenging traditional law enforcement methods. In Pakistan, the rapid digitalization of society, along with the lack of comprehensive cybersecurity frameworks, has made it particularly vulnerable to cybercrime, including hacking, identity theft and online fraud. To effectively combat these threats, international cooperation is crucial. This paper explores the need for Pakistan to build strategic partnerships with international organizations and other nations to share intelligence, resources and best practices for preventing cybercrime. By enhancing collaborations with entities like INTERPOL, the United Nations and cybersecurity-focused organizations, Pakistan can strengthen its capacity to detect, prevent and respond to cyber threats. Additionally, establishing bilateral and multilateral agreements with countries facing similar challenges can facilitate the exchange of information and technology, creating a more robust defense against cybercriminals. The paper also discusses the opportunities and challenges involved in fostering international partnerships, including legal, technical and political considerations. Ultimately, the research underscores the importance of global cooperation in safeguarding digital infrastructure and ensuring national security in an increasingly interconnected world.
