DO  - https://doi.org/10.56360/dijcm/5.ii.2025.2504
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The psychological effects of cyberbullying can be devastating.Victims often suffer from depression, anxiety, sleep disturbances, low self-esteem, academic decline, and in extreme cases, suicidal ideation (Patel, 2022).These effects are not limited to short-term distress but may persist well into adulthood if left unaddressed.Girls, in particular, appear to be more susceptible to both victimization and internalizing the trauma associated with it (Chakraborty & Roy, 2023).Moreover, cyberbullying intersects with existing social challenges in Abstract: Cyberbullying has emerged as a serious public health concern among Indian teenagers, exacerbated by rapidly expanding internet access.This study synthesizes key findings on prevalence, psychological and social consequences, contributory cultural factors, and existing mitigation strategies.Notably, cyberbullying exposure correlates with depression, anxiety, social withdrawal, physical symptoms, reduced academic performance, substance use, and-even-suicidal thoughts.Interventions pursued in India include government cyber cells, legal reforms, helplines, NGO initiatives, and institutional policies.Still, research gaps remain, particularly around effective prevention in cultural contexts.The paper concludes with recommendations for mental-health education, parental engagement, technology regulation, and future longitudinal studies.
