AU  - Shreya Roy
PY  - 2025
DO  - https://doi.org/10.18311/jcls/2025/v2i3/51401
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] This case comment analyses the Delhi High Court’s interim order passed in the case of TV Today Network Ltd. v. Google LLC, which addressed the issue of a deepfake YouTube video impersonating journalist Anjana Om Kashyap. The manipulated video was circulated, and false information was spread as accurate. The court ordered immediate take-down of the video, subscriber and revenue data disclosure, and preventive measures from any future impersonation. The court invoked Article 21 of the Constitution of India, Section 37 of the Copyright Act, and intermediary liability under the Information Technology Act. The order shows the willingness of the judiciary to adopt and extend the laws wherever possible to curb the threats of synthetic media. Also, it highlights the gap in the statutory law. This comment argues that the interim order was necessary while deciding the case, but was also an incomplete response. Comparative perspectives from the European Union, the United States, China, and South Korea have been drawn to understand how different legislation models have been drafted against using Artificial Intelligence, followed by specific recommendations India could adopt for drafting comprehensive AI Legislation. The case shows that the time has come for proper statutory law to govern the governance of threats posed by synthetic media.
