TI  - [SGINC] Exploring the Potential of Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR) as an Effective Remedy for Sextortion
AU  - Muhammad Awais Aslam
AU  - Muhammad Adil Shehroz
AU  - Ifra Batool
AU  - Iqra batool
AU  - Ishfaq Ahmad
PY  - 2025
JO  - Journal for social science archives
DO  - https://doi.org/10.59075/jssa.v3i1.138
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Sextortion, has been categorized as a form of cybercriminal activity where a person makes threats of releasing sexual details or material. In the current generation, and the future, sextortion has become rife both globally and in Pakistan. As for the traditional legal means, these are frequently ineffective in addressing such kinds of offenses, and thus, possible ways to apply ADR methods are considered. Sextortion cases make matters more complicated by the fact that victims suffer emotional, social, and psychological abuse. There are legal systems in place today to deal with blackmail and harassment but these are many a times not private, not as fast and lacking in the support that the victim might need. Perhaps, more victim-focused can be such an ‘alternative’ strategy as: The Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR) that is characterized by confidentiality, speed, and flexibility. This article explores the possibilities of ADR as solution for sextortion cases in Pakistan by reviewing the existing legislation, evaluating recent sextortion cases in the same country and comparing with the findings of other countries.
