AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] There have been significant reasons to be concerned about the behavioural and psychological outcomes of such social media addiction by university students, particularly in terms of its susceptibility to cyberbullying. The paper will present the relationship between the high usage of social media and susceptibility to cyberbullying among Indian university students using an affective cross-sectional design based on the Uses and Gratifications Theory (UGT). A total of 120 participants (100-120 respondents) between the age of 18-25 years were offered a standardized online questionnaire of patterns of using social media, the indicators of problematic use, and victimization by cyberbullying. Even the descriptive statistics revealed that more than half of the sample claimed spending four or more hours daily on their social media platforms, and that 81.6% of those sampled were moderate and high dependency users. Pearson correlation coefficients gave a strong, positive, significant correlation between excessive use of social media, and having an exposure to cyberbullying (r = 0.61, p < 0.01). Also by the simple linear regression it was demonstrated that the excessive use of the social media was considerable predictor of the vulnerability to cyberbullying and explained 37% of the variance (R2 = 0.37). The findings have demonstrated overindulgence with the purpose of gratification to result in exposure, emotional sensitivity, as well as digital hazard, which highlights the significance of social media overuse as a psychosocial threat factor. With the emphasis in preventing digital literacy programs, responsible use education, and campus-wide psychological support programmes, this paper aims at establishing safer online conditions among students.
