AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] According to the 2021 report of the National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB), Uttar Pradesh was declared the cybercrime capital of India. During COVID-19, reliance on technology paved new ways to commit cybercrime. The emerging concept of online education system had introduced children of tender age to the Internet, which in turn became an opportunity for criminals to commit crimes through online media. The NCRB report further reveals that in 2020, India registered 136 cases of cybercrime per day. In 2020, the Ministry of Home Affairs tabled a report in the Lok Sabha, which revealed that almost 30 cases were converted into first information report (FIR) out of 1,500 cybercrime cases reported every day. The registered FIR was only 2% of the registered cases. Through this study, the researchers aim to provide extensive information and procedure about reporting cybercrime through online medium and telephonic medium. Furthermore, the chapter discusses the need to report cybercrime and the lacunae in reporting mechanisms and suggests ways to improve it. The study also presents analysis of the existing cybercrimes and classifies them to develop a better understanding.
