TY  - JOUR
ID  - R1453
TI  - [SGINC] Cybercrime in Pakistan: Federal Jurisdiction, Enforcement Challenges, and Emerging Legal Responses, with Reference to the Legal Rights of Citizens
AU  - Syed Abbas Ali Shah
PY  - 2024
JO  - Social science review archives.
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The growth rate of Pakistan's digital economy has been faster than the pace of the legislative system designed to regulate it. It explores the manner in which cybercrime is regulated at the federal level, why enforcement is lagging behind what the text of the statute bills, and what citizens can expect to actually receive when their rights are at stake, be it from criminals or from the government as it fights the criminals. The study analyzes the doctrinal framework of the Constitution, the constitution of the PECA 2016 and its amendment of 2025, and the codes of the 24 substantive offence provisions of PECA 2016 by category and penalty, and by comparing the Budapest Convention with the 2024 United Nations Convention against Cybercrime. It outlines five common enforcement issues: institutional capacity, digital evidence, cross-border data, platform dependence and discretionary use. It demonstrates that the statutory penalties are weighted in favour of security and speech-related offences, and are not weighted in favour of technical intrusion and fraud (as that term is defined); that investigative powers exceed procedural safeguards; and that victims, especially women, have real life obstacles which are not addressed by formal rights. The paper ends with proposals for reform focusing on judicial oversight, data protection laws, forensic capacity and treaty engagement.
