TI  - [SGINC] Seeing is (Not) Believing: Analysing the Influence of Deepfakes on the Political Dynamics in India
AU  - Akhil Aby
PY  - 2024
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
DO  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20610147
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] The rise of AI-generated deepfakes poses a growing threat to democratic integrity, and India with its massive and digitally expanding electorate sits at a particularly dangerous crossroads. This paper investigates how deepfake technology has penetrated Indian electoral politics, drawing on news media, legislative documents, and secondary sources to map its spread and consequences. The findings reveal several documented instances in which deepfakes were deliberately deployed during Indian elections to undermine the foundational values of democratic participation. Of particular concern is the emerging phenomenon of "truth decay" — a gradual erosion of public trust in media where citizens become unable to distinguish authentic content from manipulated material. This study argues that prolonged exposure to deepfake content not only breeds widespread skepticism toward legitimate information but also creates a legal escape route for political actors and public figures seeking to evade accountability by dismissing genuine evidence as fabricated. The paper concludes with a two-pronged recommendation: adopting tighter regulatory frameworks inspired by US and EU legislation without compromising free speech, and investing in nationwide digital literacy programs that give ordinary citizens the tools to think critically about what they see online.
