ID  - R1524
TI  - [SGINC] Technology Facilitated Gender-based Violence in Low- and Middle-income Countries: Appeal for Psychosocial Response
AU  - Arjita Sharda
AU  - Madhulika Rathore
AU  - Aparna Joshi
PY  - 2024
JO  - Journal of Mental Health and Human Behaviour
DO  - https://doi.org/10.4103/jmhhb.jmhhb_172_24
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Sir, In the era of digital advancements, technology presents great opportunities for innovation, employment, education, leisure, creativity, and community building for women and yet it also introduces new vulnerabilities. Subsumed under the term technology-facilitated gender-based violence (TFGBV), (the authors acknowledge that TFGBV is often used interchangeably with technology-facilitated violence against women, technology-based abuse, technology facilitated abuse, online violence against women, online abuse, digital violence, and digital abuse), acts of gendered violence committed, assisted, aggravated, and amplified partially or fully by using information and communication technologies or digital media have become increasingly common.[1,2] TFGBV, often a continuation of offline GBV, includes behaviors such as stalking, bullying, sexual harassment, defamation, hate speech, exploitation, and gender trolling, among others.[3] Globally, 38% of women report experiencing TFGBV and 85% report witnessing it.[4] Often not isolated, TFGBV can exacerbate and multiply the experiences of existing abuse due to increased access to means and devices for surveillance, monitoring, and controlling victim survivors. The rise of digital lifestyles and technological features such as anonymity, increased chances of impunity, digital permanence, unregulated and potentially distressing content, rapid viral dissemination, global reach, and emerging technologies such as artificial intelligence (AI) are the major factors contributing to the prevalence of TFGBV.[2] Although TFGBV is a cause of concern worldwide, women in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) are impacted disproportionately. The prevalence estimates are largely under-reported and under-researched, but emerging evidence indicates that women, especially those with marginalized identities (based on caste, class, religion, sexual, and gender orientation) and those across various intersections of age and occupation are victim-survivors of TFGBV in LMICs.[3,5] In the economist intelligence unit survey (2021) on online violence against women, 62% of the countries surveyed were LMICs. Notably, these LMICs also exhibited the highest levels of internet penetration.[4] Furthermore, internet usage in the least developed countries is expanding annually by 22%, significantly outpacing the global growth rate of 7.2%.[6] This, in combination with the low levels of education, widespread patriarchal socio-cultural norms, taboos about sex and sexuality, and existing high levels of GBV make LMICs a fertile ground for TFGBV.[3] TFGBV in LMICs is further compounded by shame and victim blaming, where victim-survivors experience reputational damage and are often coerced into romantic involvement with their perpetrators.[7] In cases of TFGBV, such incidents become permanently accessible, leaving women unable to conceal or resolve them independently. This heightened visibility is likely to provoke stronger reactions from family and community members, further endangering women’s safety. The impact of experiencing TFGBV is as much a public health issue as a human rights violation. The former includes negative mental health and psychosocial consequences such as symptoms of depression, anxiety, posttraumatic stress, disordered eating and sleeping, suicide, reduced self-esteem, social isolation, low academic performance, and low productivity.[7] The latter is significant against the backdrop of disproportionate targeting of women from marginalized backgrounds (especially along the intersections of religion, gender, and sexual orientation), those who are young, those in leadership roles, and those who engage in content related to political and cultural relevance.[2,3,8] This form of violence often manifests through rape threats, misogynistic remarks, and body shaming, aimed at silencing women. Given the prevalent patriarchal and misogynistic norms in LMICs, where the internet also serves as a platform for women to bridge gaps such as accessing education, pursuing self-employment, and amplifying their voices, incidents of TFGBV deter women from engaging with technology. Consequently, it prevents women from accessing online spaces safely, contributing significantly to the digital gender divide-violating fundamental human rights and perpetuating gender inequality in LMICs. Notably, some LMICs have established legal frameworks to address online violence, such as the information technology (IT) Act of 2000 in India and the Cybercrime Act of 2015 in Nigeria. Arrests under the IT Act in cases of TFGBV have also been made.[9] However, laws require an expanded scope to explicitly address TFGBV. In addition, it is imperative that such legislation includes provisions to protect internet freedom and privacy, ensuring that it is not misused to suppress online expression. On a macro level, while initiatives such as the Global Partnership for Action on Gender Based Online Harassment and Abuse (https://www.state.gov/2023-roadmap-for-the-global-partnership-for-action-on-gender-based-online-harassment-and-abuse/) exist, representation from LMICs is woefully inadequate. Given that TFGBV often surpasses jurisdictions and country-specific laws, membership of such global and collaborative action plans will likely play a pivotal role in TFGBV prevention and intervention efforts in LMICs as well. It will be helpful to draw on existing initiatives such as the provision of an independent regulatory body like eSafety (https://www.esafety.gov.au/) in Australia. Furthermore, AI technology can also be harnessed for TFGBV prevention and intervention. AI can be utilized in ways such as application of algorithms to detect potential threats, analysis of datasets for the identification of patterns, and AI-driven chatbots for providing psycho-social support and helpline referrals. TFGBV needs to be recognized and legitimized in LMICs as an act of violence and multi-stakeholder efforts toward preventing and addressing it need to be intensified. The mental health, psychosocial, and politico-legal consequences of navigating technology and grappling with TFGBV as women in LMICs need greater research, public awareness, digital literacy and rapid training in holistic and trauma informed responses for the workforce (education, health, police, legal, justice, and mental health). Financial support and sponsorship Nil. Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts of interest.
