AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Internet is the backbone of Indian information society today. It has brought many benefits but also the threat of serious cyber attacks demonstrated over the past few years. However, cybercrimes are now executed with greater sophistication, increasingly for purposes of financial gains and in turn challenging the control technologies. Cybercrimes are also creating positive or negative jurisdiction conflicts. Even though various countries have enacted computer crime statutes that prohibit to computers. But, the definition to a computer still requires analysis and clarity. Therefore to secure privacy and protect the liberty interests of Internet users courts should reject a contract-based theory of authorization, and should limit the scope of unauthorized access statutes to circumvention of code-based restrictions on computer privileges. 
There are challenges in developing effective cross-national policing of cybercrime regarding cloud computing, social media, wireless/smart phone applications and other innovations in digital technology. Utilizing the concept of security as resilience, this paper provides India’s evolving ecosystem of cybercrime regulation? To what extent has India been able to construct a comprehensive and resilient approach to cyber security within the evolving ecosystem? The articles concludes with summarizing the various approaches for cybercrime regulations and use of technology for its prevention and control.
