AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] INTRODUCTIONLenhart, Purcell, Smith and Zickuhr (2010) noted that adolescent use of the Internet is prolific. Currently no standard definition exists for cyber victimization, but all the definitions generally contain elements of intentional and repeated harm inflicted through the use of technology (Brown C.F., Demaray M.K., and Secord S.M., 2014). Individuals who are at the receiving end of cyberbullying behaviors are considered cyber victims. Olweus (2013) defines cyberbullying as bullying performed via electronic forms of contact or communication such mobile/cell phones or the Internet. Researchers have focused upon the basic characteristics of cyberbullying to examine its definition. Willard (2007) identified and organized cyberbullying into eight different categories: flaming, harassment, denigration, cyberstalking, impersonation, outing, trickery, and exclusion.In the present study, the term cyber victimization was liked to cyberbullying, participants were required to give more solid reactions when they were asked about exploitation or victimization occurrences. Akbulut, Y., Sahin, Y. L., & Eristi, B, 2010, too utilized this comparative presumption a part of their investigation of Turkish understudies. The Indian definition for cyberbullying by Jaishankar's (2008) has been used in the study, which defined it abuse/harassment by teasing or insulting, victim's body shape, intellect, family background, dress sense, mother tongue, place of origin, attitude, race, caste, class, name calling, using modern telecommunication networks such mobile phones (SMS/MMS) and Internet (Chat rooms, emails, notice broads and groups).Theoretical FrameworkDeveloped by Marcus Felson and Lawrence Cohen (1979), the Routine Activity Theory draws upon control theories and explains crime in terms of crime opportunities occurring in everyday life and is used to examine the phenomenon of cyber victimization in the present study (Felson, Marcus, 1994). RAT has three components:1) Motivated Offender2) Target Suitability3) Capable GuardianshipMarcum (2008) reported that RAT was useful in studying adolescent cyberbullying experiences.He found that spending moretime online communicating and sharing personal information online increased the likelihood of being victimized online. As per Hinduja & Patchin, 2007; Beran & Li, 2007, only a few studies have been carried out in this area and hence, effective measures to deal with the problem have not yet been discovered. Navarro and Jasinski (2011) reported RAT's success in explaining cyberbullying, they discovered that people who utilized SNS had a higher rate of cyberbullying (39.4%) than individuals who were cyberbullied (22.5%) elsewhere.The present work aims to extend this theory to cyber victimization and understand if online activities or the online environment itself provides opportunities for crime to occur online. And also, how certain individuals are at a greater risk for being victimized online in the hands of predators (Cohen, Lawrence & Marcus Felson, 1979).The first variable is a given inclination per the theory and hence, is not put to test. By means of three questionnaires developed for the study, first, the victims were distinguished from the non-victims of cyber victimization. Two research questions are focused upon:1) Will victims and non-victims differ on parental supervision, target suitability and their dimensions?2) Will victims and non-victims predict parental supervision, target suitability and its dimensions?METHODParticipantsThe sample comprised of 200 adolescents (Male=120, Female=80; Mean age=16.89, SD=0.653) within the age range 16-18 years studying in grades 11th and 12th. The one's who were internet users, fluent in English language and were able to provide consent for the study were included for the study.MATERIALS AND PROCEDUREThe following questionnaires were developed for the study. …
