ID  - R2071
TI  - [SGINC] Indonesia's data protection Regulation 2012: A brief code with data breach notification
AU  - Graham Greenleaf
AU  - Sinta Dewi Rosadi
PY  - 2013
JO  - SSRN Electronic Journal
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Indonesia is usually ignored in discussions of data privacy, but as an Asian democracy with a population of over 250 million (exceeded only by China and India) and a fast-developing economy with 6.2 % economic growth in 20121, its position is important to data privacy in Asia. Although it is the largest ASEAN state, it has moved slowly to provide comprehensive legal protection for personal information, and is now lagging behind its ASEAN neighbours, Singapore, Malaysia and the Philippines, all of which now have data protection laws covering much of their private sectors (and the public sector, in the case of the Philippines). The absence of comprehensive data protection legislation in Indonesia is believed to have contributed to trading of personal data by insurance companies, banks and mobile phone service providers for telemarketing, and has created widespread public distrust. Although Indonesia has not yet gone down the path of comprehensive legislation, it has in late 2012 enacted a Regulation under its 2008 law on elec-tronic transactions (previously dormant in relation to data protection but active in some areas such as cybercrime), adding components of a brief but enforceable privacy code, and a data breach notification requirement. These laws are of somewhat limited scope, applying only to 'electronic system operators' and to actions through electronic media, somewhat similar to China. Indonesian scholars and privacy advocates do not regard this reform as sufficient, because the regulation is too broad and unclear, as well as limited in scope. This article discusses the new Regulation, and puts it in context with other aspects of data protection in Indonesia.
