TI  - [SGINC] A Comparison of Cyber-Crime Definitions in India and the United States
AU  - Himanshu Maheshwari
AU  - Harvey Hyman
AU  - Manish Agrawal
PY  - 2011
JO  - Advances in digital crime, forensics, and cyber terrorism book series
DO  - https://doi.org/10.4018/978-1-60960-123-2.ch003
AB  - [SGINC: on-topic] Unlike traditional crimes, it is difficult to define legal jurisdiction and authority for prosecuting cyber crimes. This issue is further complicated by differences in definitions of cyber crime in different countries. This chapter motivates the issue with an example of the ILOVEYOU virus and compares the legal provisions to combat cyber-crime in the US and India. The authors find that there are significant differences between India and the US in definitions of cybercrimes. It appears that in the United States, it is a crime to access information that has been declared to be confidential. In India, criminality requires dissemination of the information obtained without authorization. Another notable difference between the prosecutions of cybercrimes in the two countries relates to obscenity and decency laws.
